---@meta
-- The types of the server's options, for lua-language-server. Written by
-- scripts/generate-docs.mjs from `org-lsp settings`; edit
-- server/org-lsp/src/settings.rs, not this file.

--- The server's options, as `settings` passes them; see docs/configuration.org.
---@class org_lsp.Settings
---@field config_file? false|string The shared configuration file, whose options this editor's go on top of: `false` for none, or the path of another file (JSON if it ends in `.json`, else TOML). Unset, it is `$ORG_LSP_CONFIG`, else `config.toml` or `config.json` in `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/org-lsp` (`~/.config/org-lsp`; `%APPDATA%\org-lsp` on Windows).
---@field org_agenda_files? string[] Files, directories and glob patterns (`*`, `?`, `**`) the agenda is built from. `~/` is the home directory, and a directory stands for the Org files directly in it. When empty, the agenda is built from the Org files under the workspace folders (hidden directories, `node_modules` and `target` left out), or else from the Org files that are open. (`org-agenda-files`)
---@field org_agenda_file_regexp? string Which files of a directory in `org_agenda_files` are agenda files: an Emacs regular expression their names match, such as `\\.txt\\'` for the names that end in `.txt`. (`org-agenda-file-regexp`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_unavailable_files? boolean Leave out the files of `org_agenda_files` that are not there. With `false`, opening an agenda view asks about each: `r` removes it from the list until the server starts again, anything else aborts. (`org-agenda-skip-unavailable-files`)
---@field org_todo_keywords? string[]|string[][] The TODO sequence, written like Org's `#+TODO:` line. `|` separates the states that count as done; without it the last keyword is the only done state. `TODO(t)` gives a keyword a key for fast selection, and `WAIT(w@/!)` makes it log a note (`@`) or the time (`!`) on entering and leaving it. Several sequences go in a list of lists, `[["TODO", "|", "DONE"], ["REPORT", "|", "FIXED"]]`; cycling stays in the sequence of the entry, and `C-S-<right>` switches to the next. A list that starts with `type` is a type sequence (`#+TYP_TODO:`): each of its keywords goes straight to done. A file's own `#+TODO:` lines take precedence. (`org-todo-keywords`)
---@field org_todo_interpretation? 'sequence'|'type' What a list of keywords alone in `org_todo_keywords` is (Org's old syntax): a sequence, or types, each going straight to done. A list that starts with `sequence` or `type` says it itself. (`org-todo-interpretation`)
---@field org_todo_keyword_faces? table Faces for particular TODO keywords: a color, `{ "WAIT": "orange" }`, or attributes written as Org writes them, `{ "CANCELLED": ":foreground gray :slant italic :strike-through t" }` (`:foreground`, `:background`, `:weight bold`, `:slant italic`, `:underline t`, `:strike-through t`). Shown in Org files and in the agenda by Neovim, Vim and VS Code. (`org-todo-keyword-faces`)
---@field org_priority_faces? table Faces for particular priorities, by their letter, written as in `org_todo_keyword_faces`: `{ "A": ":foreground red :weight bold" }`. (`org-priority-faces`)
---@field org_tag_faces? table Faces for particular tags of headlines, written as in `org_todo_keyword_faces`: `{ "work": "orange" }`. Shown in Org files (not the agenda, as in Emacs) by Neovim, Vim and VS Code. (`org-tag-faces`)
---@field org_log_done? false|'time'|'note' Write `CLOSED:` with the time when an entry is done, and with `note` ask for a note too. A file's `#+STARTUP:` words `logdone`, `lognotedone` and `nologdone` change it there; an entry's `LOGGING` property (inherited) says what its state changes log instead: those words, `logrepeat` and its relatives, and keywords with what they log (`WAIT(@/!)`), or `nil` for nothing. (`org-log-done`)
---@field org_log_repeat? false|'time'|'note' Record when a repeating entry is done and moves to its next date. `#+STARTUP:` `logrepeat`, `lognoterepeat` and `nologrepeat` change it in a file, and the `LOGGING` property in an entry. (`org-log-repeat`)
---@field org_M_RET_may_split_line? boolean|table Inserting a heading, an item or a table row (M-RET) in the middle of a line splits it, the rest going to the new one; `false`: it goes in after the line. One by context: `{ "headline": false, "item": true, "table": true }`, `default` for those not named. (`org-M-RET-may-split-line`)
---@field org_blank_before_new_entry? table Whether a new heading (`heading`) and a new item (`plain_list_item`) get an empty line before them: `true`, `false`, or `"auto"`, as the headings or items around them have. (`org-blank-before-new-entry`)
---@field org_insert_heading_respect_content? boolean M-RET and M-S-RET outside lists put the new heading after the subtree, as C-RET does. (`org-insert-heading-respect-content`)
---@field org_special_ctrl_a_e? true|false|'reversed'|(true|false|'reversed')[] How C-a and C-e (`org.beginning.of.line`, `org.end.of.line`) move on a headline or an item: `true` to the start of the headline's title, after the stars, TODO keyword and priority, or after the item's bullet and check-box, and to the end of the title, before the tags; the same key again to the line's own start or end. `reversed` goes to the line's start or end first, and to those places on the same key right after. A pair, as `[true, false]`, sets C-a and C-e apart. With `false` the key keeps the editor's own meaning. (`org-special-ctrl-a/e`)
---@field org_sparse_tree_open_archived_trees? boolean A sparse tree (`C-c /`, and a link's `/regexp/`) shows its matches in archived subtrees (tagged `ARCHIVE`); with `false` those subtrees stay folded under their headline. (`org-sparse-tree-open-archived-trees`)
---@field org_occur_case_fold_search? true|false|'smart' Whether a sparse tree (`C-c /`, and a link's `/regexp/`) ignores case; `"smart"` ignores it unless what is searched for has an upper-case letter. (`org-occur-case-fold-search`)
---@field org_highlight_sparse_tree_matches? boolean Highlight the matches of a sparse tree (`C-c /`, and a link's `/regexp/`) until the text changes; with `false`, only their lines show. (`org-highlight-sparse-tree-matches`)
---@field org_remove_highlights_with_change? boolean The highlights of a sparse tree (`C-c /` by a regexp, TODO keywords or dates, and a link's `/regexp/`) and the times of `C-c C-x C-d` go at the next change of the text; with `false` they stay until `C-c C-c`. Those of a sparse tree by tags or a property always stay, as in Emacs. (`org-remove-highlights-with-change`)
---@field org_sparse_tree_default_date_type? null|'all'|'active'|'inactive'|'scheduled'|'deadline'|'closed' The timestamps the date sparse trees (`C-c /` `b`, `a`, `D`) look at first: `null` for scheduled and deadline dates, or one kind; `c` in the menu turns to the next kind. (`org-sparse-tree-default-date-type`)
---@field org_yank_adjusted_subtrees? boolean C-y (`org.yank`, Emacs keys) of one or more subtrees at the start of a line, or after the stars of an otherwise empty headline, pastes them as `org.paste.subtree` does: at the level of the headlines around them. Anything else is the editor's own paste. (`org-yank-adjusted-subtrees`)
---@field org_yank_folded_subtrees? boolean C-y (`org.yank`, Emacs keys) of one or more subtrees at the start of a line folds them, unless that would hide text after them (*Inserted text not folded because that would swallow text*), and leaves the cursor on the line after them. With `false` (and `org_yank_adjusted_subtrees` off) it is the editor's own paste. (`org-yank-folded-subtrees`)
---@field org_allow_promoting_top_level_subtree? boolean Promoting a headline of level 1 makes it a comment line: `# ` in place of `* `, its children a level up with a subtree's promotion. With `false` it is an error. (`org-allow-promoting-top-level-subtree`)
---@field org_odd_levels_only? boolean Only odd levels: `*`, `***`, `*****`, each counted as the next level. Promoting and demoting go two stars at a time, and a headline is colored and matched (`LEVEL`) by the level it counts as. `#+STARTUP: odd` and `oddeven` set it for a file. (`org-odd-levels-only`)
---@field org_inlinetask_min_level? false|integer A headline of at least this many stars (twice as many less one with `org_odd_levels_only`) is an inline task: a task inside the text of its entry, closed by a line of as many stars and `END`. It is no part of the outline: it has no subtree, the outline and the motion between headings pass it, and it folds its text up to its `END` line. `false`: there are no inline tasks, as in Emacs before `org-inlinetask` is loaded. (`org-inlinetask-min-level`)
---@field org_inlinetask_show_first_star? boolean The stars of an inline task but the last two are hidden, as `org_hide_leading_stars` hides those of a headline; with `true` none are. (`org-inlinetask-show-first-star`)
---@field org_inlinetask_default_state? string The TODO keyword of a new inline task (`org.inlinetask.insert`). (`org-inlinetask-default-state`)
---@field org_treat_S_cursor_todo_selection_as_state_change? boolean `S-<left>` and `S-<right>` on a headline change its TODO state as any change does: logged, and blocked by its dependencies. Off, they change it with nothing logged and nothing in the way. (`org-treat-S-cursor-todo-selection-as-state-change`)
---@field org_treat_insert_todo_heading_as_state_change? boolean The keyword of a heading `org.insert.todo.heading` (M-S-RET) makes is set as a TODO change: logged as the keyword says (a note wanted is a time only), with its tag triggers. (`org-treat-insert-todo-heading-as-state-change`)
---@field org_edit_timestamp_down_means_later? boolean `S-<down>` makes a timestamp later and `S-<up>` earlier, the other way round from Org's default. (`org-edit-timestamp-down-means-later`)
---@field org_priority_enable_commands? boolean Whether the priority commands work. With `false` they refuse (`Priority commands are disabled`), and `S-<up>` and `S-<down>` on a headline do not change its priority. (`org-priority-enable-commands`)
---@field org_track_ordered_property_with_tag? boolean|string A tag that `org.ordered.toggle` (`C-c C-x o`) puts on the headline with the `ORDERED` property and takes off without it: `true` for `ORDERED`, or a tag of your own. Only the property orders the subtasks. (`org-track-ordered-property-with-tag`)
---@field org_special_ctrl_o? boolean `org.open.line` (C-o) in a table inserts a row above; with `false` it opens a line there too. (`org-special-ctrl-o`)
---@field org_special_ctrl_k? boolean C-k (`org.kill.line`) on a headline kills the text to the tags, and the tags when the cursor is past the title; at the start of the line, the whole line. With `false` the key keeps the editor's own meaning, but on a line a closed fold starts on (see `org_ctrl_k_protect_subtree`). (`org-special-ctrl-k`)
---@field org_ctrl_k_protect_subtree? false|true|'error' C-k (`org.kill.line`) on a line a closed fold starts on (a headline's only from its start with `org_special_ctrl_k`) kills what the fold hides with the line, as Emacs's `kill-line` does: `true` asks first (*Kill hidden subtree along with headline?*), `"error"` refuses (*‘C-k’ aborted as it would kill a hidden subtree*), `false` kills. Needs an editor that says which folds are closed (not VS Code). (`org-ctrl-k-protect-subtree`)
---@field org_list_allow_alphabetical? boolean Whether a letter may stand for the number of an ordered item (`a.`, `B)`). A list of more than 26 items is numbered. (`org-list-allow-alphabetical`)
---@field org_plain_list_ordered_item_terminator? true|'.'|')' What ends the number of an ordered item: `"."`, `")"`, or `true` for either. (`org-plain-list-ordered-item-terminator`)
---@field org_list_two_spaces_after_bullet_regexp? false|string An Emacs regexp: the bullets it matches get two spaces after them when Org writes them (a new item, a bullet changed, a list repaired). (`org-list-two-spaces-after-bullet-regexp`)
---@field org_list_demote_modify_bullet? table By bullet (`-`, `+`, `*`, `1.`, `1)`), the bullet an item gets when it is indented: `{ "+": "-", "-": "+" }`. (`org-list-demote-modify-bullet`)
---@field org_list_indent_offset? integer How many more columns sub-items are indented, past their parent's bullet. (`org-list-indent-offset`)
---@field org_list_automatic_rules? table `checkbox`: the statistics cookies are counted again when a box changes, and colored as done or not. `indent`: indenting the first item of a list with its children (M-S-<right>) moves the whole list. A rule left out of the object is off. (`org-list-automatic-rules`)
---@field org_checkbox_hierarchical_statistics? boolean A checkbox cookie counts the boxes of the items right below it; `false` counts every box further down too, as `recursive` in a `COOKIE_DATA` property does. (`org-checkbox-hierarchical-statistics`)
---@field org_table_number_regexp? string An Emacs regexp: the fields it matches are numbers, and a column mostly of numbers aligns right. (`org-table-number-regexp`)
---@field org_table_number_fraction? number How much of a column's filled fields must be numbers for it to align right. (`org-table-number-fraction`)
---@field org_table_automatic_realign? boolean TAB and RET in a table align it before they move; `false`: only C-c C-c and the commands that change rows or columns do. (`org-table-automatic-realign`)
---@field org_table_tab_jumps_over_hlines? boolean TAB at the end of a row before a horizontal line goes to the row after it; `false`: it makes a row before the line. (`org-table-tab-jumps-over-hlines`)
---@field org_table_shrunk_column_indicator? string What shows at the end of each field of a shrunk column. (`org-table-shrunk-column-indicator`)
---@field org_table_formula_constants? table Constants for the table formulas of every file, by name: `{ "c": "299792458." }` gives `$c`. A file's `#+CONSTANTS:` come first. (`org-table-formula-constants`)
---@field org_table_relative_ref_may_cross_hline? true|false|'error' Whether a relative reference in a formula (`@-1`) goes past a horizontal line: `false` stops at the field next to it, `"error"` makes the formula fail. (`org-table-relative-ref-may-cross-hline`)
---@field org_table_formula_create_columns? boolean A field formula past the last column adds the columns it needs, rather than failing. (`org-table-formula-create-columns`)
---@field org_table_duration_custom_format? 'seconds'|'minutes'|'hours'|'days'|'hh:mm' How a formula with `;t` writes a duration: a number of seconds, minutes, hours or days, or hours and minutes. (`org-table-duration-custom-format`)
---@field org_table_duration_hour_zero_padding? boolean Durations of formulas (`;T`, `;U`) have their hours on two digits: `08:32:34` rather than `8:32:34`. (`org-table-duration-hour-zero-padding`)
---@field org_calc_default_modes? table The modes table formulas start with: `float_format`, `["float", digits]`, `["fix", decimals]` or `["sci", digits]`, and `angle_mode`, `"deg"` or `"rad"`. A formula's own modes (`;f2`) win; Calc's other modes are not offered. (`org-calc-default-modes`)
---@field org_table_formula_evaluate_inline? boolean TAB and RET on a field that is a formula (`=$1*2`, `:=...`) evaluate it, which org-lsp refuses (the formula goes in a `#+TBLFM:` line); `false` leaves the field as text. (`org-table-formula-evaluate-inline`)
---@field org_table_allow_automatic_line_recalculation? boolean TAB, RET and C-c C-c on a line marked `#` recalculate it. (`org-table-allow-automatic-line-recalculation`)
---@field org_list_use_circular_motion? boolean Moving to the next item from the last goes to the first, and back; moving the last item down puts it first, the first up puts it last. (`org-list-use-circular-motion`)
---@field org_enforce_todo_dependencies? boolean An entry cannot be done while a child is not, or, under a parent with the `ORDERED` property, while an earlier sibling is not: the change is refused with what blocks it. (`org-enforce-todo-dependencies`)
---@field org_agenda_dim_blocked_tasks? true|false|'invisible' Dim the tasks of the agenda and the TODO list that their dependencies keep from being done (with `org_enforce_todo_dependencies` or `org_enforce_todo_checkbox_dependencies`); `invisible` leaves them out instead, except those blocked by checkboxes, which stay dimmed. (`org-agenda-dim-blocked-tasks`)
---@field org_enforce_todo_checkbox_dependencies? boolean An entry cannot be done while it has an unchecked box. (`org-enforce-todo-checkbox-dependencies`)
---@field org_log_refile? false|'time'|'note' Record when an entry is refiled (`Refiled on [2026-05-18 Mon 10:30]`), with `note` asking for a note too. `#+STARTUP:` `logrefile`, `lognoterefile` and `nologrefile` change it in the file refiled to. (`org-log-refile`)
---@field org_log_reschedule? false|'time'|'note' Record when a scheduled date changes or is removed (`Rescheduled from "[2026-05-18 Mon]" on …`), with `note` asking for a note too. `#+STARTUP:` `logreschedule`, `lognotereschedule` and `nologreschedule` change it in a file. (`org-log-reschedule`)
---@field org_log_redeadline? false|'time'|'note' Record when a deadline changes or is removed (`New deadline from …`), with `note` asking for a note too. `#+STARTUP:` `logredeadline`, `lognoteredeadline` and `nologredeadline` change it in a file. (`org-log-redeadline`)
---@field org_log_into_drawer? boolean|string Put state changes and notes into a drawer: `true` for `LOGBOOK`, or the name of a drawer. `#+STARTUP:` `logdrawer` and `nologdrawer` change it in a file; an entry's `LOG_INTO_DRAWER` property (inherited) says which drawer for it (`t` for `LOGBOOK`, `nil` for none). (`org-log-into-drawer`)
---@field org_log_done_with_time? boolean The `CLOSED:` timestamp of an entry done has the time, not only the date. (`org-log-done-with-time`)
---@field org_log_note_headings? table The heading of each kind of note, by kind: `done` (`CLOSING NOTE %t`), `state` (`State %-12s from %-12S %t`), `note`, `reschedule`, `delschedule`, `redeadline`, `deldeadline`, `refile` and `clock_out`. In a heading `%t` is the time, `%T` the time active, `%d` and `%D` the date, `%s` the new state or date and `%S` the old one, `%u` the login name and `%U` the full name; an empty heading leaves the note its text only. Kinds left out keep Org's headings. Habits take notes with the `done` heading for days done. (`org-log-note-headings`)
---@field org_log_states_order_reversed? boolean Put a new state note, note or clock line above the others (`false`: below them). `#+STARTUP:` `logstatesreversed` and `nologstatesreversed` change it in a file. (`org-log-states-order-reversed`)
---@field org_log_state_notes_insert_after_drawers? boolean Without a log drawer, put notes after the drawers right under the headline and planning line, not before them. (`org-log-state-notes-insert-after-drawers`)
---@field org_log_note_clock_out? boolean Ask for a note when the clock stops (`org.clock.out`), which goes right after the clock line. `#+STARTUP:` `lognoteclock-out` and `nolognoteclock-out` change it in a file. (`org-log-note-clock-out`)
---@field org_provide_todo_statistics? boolean|'all-headlines'|string[]|string[][] Update the `[1/3]` and `[33%]` cookies of the parent when a state changes, counting the entries with a keyword (`true`), every entry (`all-headlines`, those without a keyword as not done), those with these keywords and the done ones (a list), or those of the first list as not done and of the second as done (two lists). `false` leaves the cookies. (`org-provide-todo-statistics`)
---@field org_hierarchical_todo_statistics? boolean TODO statistics count the children only; `false`: the whole subtree, and the cookies above too. A `COOKIE_DATA` property with `recursive` does this for a subtree. (`org-hierarchical-todo-statistics`)
---@field org_closed_keep_when_no_todo? boolean Keep the `CLOSED:` timestamp when an entry is left with no keyword. (`org-closed-keep-when-no-todo`)
---@field org_todo_state_tags_triggers? table Tags that change with the state, by state: a keyword, `""` for none, `todo` for any not done, `done` for any done; each a list of tags to set (`+TAG`) or remove (`-TAG`), in order: `{ "WAIT": ["+waiting"], "todo": ["-done"], "done": ["+done", "-waiting"] }`. (`org-todo-state-tags-triggers`)
---@field org_tags_column? integer Where tags are aligned after a change to a headline. Negative: they end at that column. (`org-tags-column`)
---@field org_use_tag_inheritance? boolean|string[]|string Which tags entries inherit from their parents and from `#+FILETAGS:`: `true` for all, `false` for none, a list of tags, or a regexp (Emacs syntax) tags must match. Inherited tags count in tag searches and show in the agenda. (`org-use-tag-inheritance`)
---@field org_tags_exclude_from_inheritance? string[] Tags never inherited, when `org_use_tag_inheritance` is `true` or a regexp. (`org-tags-exclude-from-inheritance`)
---@field org_use_property_inheritance? boolean|string[]|string Which properties an entry without them takes from its parents, the file's property drawer and `#+PROPERTY:` lines, in property searches (`OWNER="alice"`): `true` for all, `false` for none, a list of names, or a regexp (Emacs syntax). `CATEGORY` and `ARCHIVE` are always inherited. (`org-use-property-inheritance`)
---@field org_property_separators? any[][] What joins the values of a property with those of its `PROPERTY+` lines, and the inherited ones, instead of a space: `[["AUTHOR", "COAUTHOR"], ", "]` for the properties named, `["^TAG", ";"]` for those an Emacs regexp matches, without regard to case. The first that fits counts. (`org-property-separators`)
---@field org_property_format? string How a property line is written when it is indented (TAB, `org.indent`) or set: `format`'s `%s` for the key with its colons, then the value, with a width (`%-10s` pads the key to 10 columns). (`org-property-format`)
---@field org_global_properties? table Properties every entry inherits, after those of `#+PROPERTY:` lines: chiefly the allowed values of a property, which setting it offers and `S-<right>` and `S-<left>` go through on its line (`{ "Effort_ALL": "0:10 0:30 1:00 2:00" }`; `:ETC` allows others too). (`org-global-properties`)
---@field org_clock_into_drawer? boolean|string Where `CLOCK:` lines go: `true` for the drawer of `org_log_into_drawer` (`LOGBOOK` when that is not a name), the name of a drawer, or `false` for the entry itself. (`org-clock-into-drawer`)
---@field org_clock_out_remove_zero_time_clocks? boolean Clocking out after less than a minute removes the `CLOCK:` line. (`org-clock-out-remove-zero-time-clocks`)
---@field org_clock_out_when_done? boolean Marking the clocked entry done stops the clock. (`org-clock-out-when-done`)
---@field org_clock_display_default_range? 'today'|'yesterday'|'thisweek'|'lastweek'|'thismonth'|'lastmonth'|'thisyear'|'lastyear'|'untilnow' The span whose clocks `org.clock.display` (`C-c C-x C-d`) adds up. (`org-clock-display-default-range`)
---@field org_clock_idle_time? integer After how many minutes without a message from the editor a clock that runs asks what to do with the idle time: keep it, subtract it (and clock out), or cancel the clock. (`org-clock-idle-time`)
---@field org_clock_auto_clock_resolution? true|false|'when-no-clock-is-running' Clocking in first asks about each clock left running in the open and agenda files, as `org.clock.resolve` does: `when-no-clock-is-running` when no clock runs, `true` always (the clock that runs too), `false` never. A clock found running when the server starts, or written by hand, is no clock the server ran: it is asked about. (`org-clock-auto-clock-resolution`)
---@field org_clock_resolve_expert? boolean Ask what to do with a dangling clock or idle time with the keys in the prompt alone (`[jkKtTgGSscCiq]?`), without the list of what each one does. (`org-clock-resolve-expert`)
---@field org_clock_history_length? integer How many entries clocked into the clock history keeps, for `org.clock.in` with a count and `org.clock.in.last`. (`org-clock-history-length`)
---@field org_clock_in_resume? boolean Clocking into an entry whose `CLOCK:` line was left without an end goes on with that clock, from when it started, rather than starting a new line. (`org-clock-in-resume`)
---@field org_clock_persist? true|false|'clock'|'history' Keep the running clock and the clock history when the server stops, and take them back when it starts: `true` both, `clock` the running clock alone, `history` the history alone (what `C-u C-c C-x C-i` offers). The running clock runs again if its `CLOCK:` line is still open. They are kept in Emacs's own file, in its form, so Emacs and org-lsp take the clock from each other. (`org-clock-persist`)
---@field org_clock_persist_file? string The file `org_clock_persist` keeps the clock in: Emacs's, `org-clock-save.el` in its directory. (`org-clock-persist-file`)
---@field org_clock_persist_query_resume? boolean Ask before the clock the last session kept runs again (`Resume clock (…)?`). (`org-clock-persist-query-resume`)
---@field org_clock_goto_may_find_recent_task? boolean With no clock running, `org.clock.goto` goes to the entry clocked last. With `false`, it says `No active or recent clock task`. (`org-clock-goto-may-find-recent-task`)
---@field org_clock_clocktable_default_properties? string The parameters a new clock table gets on its `#+BEGIN:` line from `org.clock.report` (`C-c C-x C-r`), after `:scope`, as Emacs Lisp reads them (`:maxlevel 3 :link t`). (`org-clock-clocktable-default-properties`)
---@field org_clocktable_defaults? string The parameters every clock table has unless its `#+BEGIN:` line gives them, as Emacs Lisp reads them. Set, it replaces Org's whole list. (`org-clocktable-defaults`)
---@field org_clock_clocktable_language_setup? string[][] The words of a clock table in each language its `:lang` names: the language, then its words for `File`, `L`, `Timestamp`, `Headline`, `Time`, `ALL`, `Total time`, `File time` and `Clock summary at`. A word left out stays English. Set, it replaces Org's languages. (`org-clock-clocktable-language-setup`)
---@field org_clock_report_include_clocking_task? boolean A clock table counts the time of the clock that runs, for its entry, when the table covers a time (`:block`, `:tstart` and `:tend`) and the clock started within it. (`org-clock-report-include-clocking-task`)
---@field org_clock_rounding_minutes? integer Clocking in and out happens at the multiple of this many minutes nearest now; a clock never starts later than now. Below 1, at the minute. (`org-clock-rounding-minutes`)
---@field org_clock_continuously? boolean A clock starts when the last one stopped, not now. (`org-clock-continuously`)
---@field org_clock_total_time_cell_format? string How a clock table writes its total time and the words before it; `%s` is the text. (`org-clock-total-time-cell-format`)
---@field org_clock_file_time_cell_format? string How a clock table over several files writes the words before a file's time; `%s` is the text. (`org-clock-file-time-cell-format`)
---@field org_clock_in_switch_to_state? string The TODO state an entry is put in when the clock starts on it (`NEXT`), unless it has it already. (`org-clock-in-switch-to-state`)
---@field org_clock_out_switch_to_state? string The TODO state an entry is put in when the clock stops on it. (`org-clock-out-switch-to-state`)
---@field org_lint? boolean Org's linter checks the documents and reports what it finds as diagnostics: misplaced headings and planning lines, links to nowhere, duplicate names, footnotes without definitions… What it finds is information, a hint for a checker Org trusts little: an Org file is text, nothing in it is an error or a warning. (`org-lint`)
---@field org_lint_ignore? string[] The `org-lint` checkers to leave out, by name, as a diagnostic's code shows it (`"misplaced-heading"`). (`org-lint`)
---@field org_id_method? 'uuid'|'uuidgen'|'org'|'ts' How a new `ID` is made (`org.id.create`, a link stored by ID, org-roam): a random UUID, the time in base 36 (`org`), or the time as `org_id_ts_format` writes it (`ts`). (`org-id-method`)
---@field org_id_prefix? false|string What a new `ID` starts with, before a colon: `"Org"` makes `Org:…`. (`org-id-prefix`)
---@field org_id_ts_format? string How the `ts` method writes the time, as `format-time-string` does. (`org-id-ts-format`)
---@field org_id_include_domain? boolean The `org` and `ts` methods end a new `ID` with `@` and the machine's domain. (`org-id-include-domain`)
---@field org_id_track_globally? boolean IDs are looked for in other files than the link's own: those of `org_id_search_archives`, `org_id_extra_files` and `org_id_locations_file`, the agenda files, the open ones and org-roam's. Off, an `id:` link leads only within its file. (`org-id-track-globally`)
---@field org_id_search_archives? boolean IDs are looked for in the archive files of the agenda files too. (`org-id-search-archives`)
---@field org_id_extra_files? string[] More files to look for IDs in: paths or glob patterns. (`org-id-extra-files`)
---@field org_id_locations_file? string The file where Emacs keeps the files it found IDs in: org-lsp looks in them too, and never writes it. Names in it that are not absolute are relative to its directory. (`org-id-locations-file`)
---@field org_id_completion_targets? org_lsp.RefileTarget[] The headlines an `id:` link completes to (`org.link.insert`), as `org_refile_targets` names them: by default every headline of the file and of the files with IDs. (`org-id-completion-targets`)
---@field org_link_search_must_match_exact_headline? true|false|'query-to-create' What a link to a headline that is not there does: `"query-to-create"` asks whether to make it, at the end of the file, when the link is followed (`org.open`); `true` is an error; `false` looks for its words in the text. (`org-link-search-must-match-exact-headline`)
---@field org_link_doi_server_url? string What a `doi:` link is opened at: this, then the DOI. (`org-link-doi-server-url`)
---@field org_link_shell_skip_confirm_regexp? string An Emacs regexp: a `shell:` link whose command it matches runs when followed without asking `Execute … in shell?` first. Empty asks for every command. (`org-link-shell-skip-confirm-regexp`)
---@field org_man_command? string The program that writes the man page of a `man:` link, when `ol-man` is in `org_modules`: the page is shown as text, at the line before the first match of what follows `::` (`man:ls::-l`). `"woman"`, Emacs's own reader, is `man` too. (`org-man-command`)
---@field org_info_other_documents? table The pages on the web of Info manuals: following an `info:` link to one of them, or to one of Emacs's manuals, opens its page at the node. Another manual is shown as text, as the `info` program writes the node. (`org-info-other-documents`)
---@field org_ctags_path_to_ctags? string The `ctags` program (Exuberant or Universal Ctags) that `org.ctags.create` runs to make the `TAGS` file of a directory, when `org-ctags` is in `org_modules`. (`org-ctags-path-to-ctags`)
---@field org_ctags_open_link_functions? ('org-ctags-find-tag'|'org-ctags-ask-rebuild-tags-file-then-find-tag'|'org-ctags-rebuild-tags-file-then-find-tag'|'org-ctags-ask-append-topic'|'org-ctags-append-topic'|'org-ctags-ask-visit-buffer-or-file'|'org-ctags-visit-buffer-or-file'|'org-ctags-fail-silently')[] With `org-ctags` in `org_modules`, what following a link to a headline, a target or a custom ID tries first, in turn, until one is done: `org-ctags-find-tag` (the tag of the `TAGS` file of the file's directory), `org-ctags-ask-rebuild-tags-file-then-find-tag` and `org-ctags-rebuild-tags-file-then-find-tag` (the `TAGS` file made again, then the tag), `org-ctags-ask-append-topic` and `org-ctags-append-topic` (a new topic at the end of the file), `org-ctags-ask-visit-buffer-or-file` and `org-ctags-visit-buffer-or-file` (the Org file of that name in the directory, made when asked), `org-ctags-fail-silently` (nothing more). When none is done, the link is followed as without them. (`org-ctags-open-link-functions`)
---@field org_ctags_new_topic_template? string The text of a new topic `org_ctags_open_link_functions` makes, `%t` the link's text capitalized. (`org-ctags-new-topic-template`)
---@field org_id_link_to_org_use_id? true|false|'create-if-interactive'|'create-if-interactive-and-no-custom-id'|'use-existing' Whether `org.link.store` (`C-c l`) links to an entry by its ID: `true` makes one when the entry has none, `"create-if-interactive"` only from the command (not for capture's `%a`), `"create-if-interactive-and-no-custom-id"` likewise unless the entry has a `CUSTOM_ID`, `"use-existing"` only when it has one already. Off, the link searches for the headline. (`org-id-link-to-org-use-id`)
---@field org_link_context_for_files? boolean Links to a place in a file search for it (`file:notes.org::*Heading`); `C-u` turns it around for one link. (`org-link-context-for-files`)
---@field org_id_link_use_context? boolean ID links to a place below the entry's headline search for it as well (`id:…::name`). (`org-id-link-use-context`)
---@field org_id_link_consider_parent_id? boolean An ID link may use the ID of a parent entry, with a search for the place. (`org-id-link-consider-parent-id`)
---@field org_link_file_path_type? 'adaptive'|'absolute'|'noabbrev'|'relative' How links to files write the path: `"adaptive"` relative below the file's directory and absolute elsewhere, `"absolute"` (with `~`), `"noabbrev"` (without), `"relative"`. (`org-link-file-path-type`)
---@field org_link_keep_stored_after_insertion? boolean Stored links stay stored once inserted. (`org-link-keep-stored-after-insertion`)
---@field org_complete_tags_always_offer_all_agenda_tags? boolean Completing a tag offers the tags of every agenda file, after those of the file. Off, only the file's own. Setting tags offers them too. (`org-complete-tags-always-offer-all-agenda-tags`)
---@field org_cite_global_bibliography? string[] Bibliography files for the citations of every document (`[cite:@key]`), after those its `#+BIBLIOGRAPHY:` lines name: BibTeX (`.bib`, `.bibtex`) or CSL-JSON (`.json`). Their entries are completed after `@`, shown over a key, and where following a key goes; a key none has is marked. (`org-cite-global-bibliography`)
---@field org_cite_insert_processor? string|false The citation processor `org.cite.insert` inserts with: `basic`, as Org's own does; Org's other processors cannot insert, and those of packages (citar) are not run; `false` for none. (`org-cite-insert-processor`)
---@field org_bibtex_autogen_keys? boolean Whether `org.bibtex.create` and `org.bibtex.check` make the key of a new BibTeX entry from its author, year and title, as `bibtex-generate-autokey` makes it (through Emacs), instead of asking for it (`id`). (`org-bibtex-autogen-keys`)
---@field org_bibtex_prefix? string What the names of the properties the BibTeX commands write start with (`BIB_` gives `BIB_AUTHOR`), but for the key's; they read the names with it and without. (`org-bibtex-prefix`)
---@field org_bibtex_treat_headline_as_title? boolean A headline without a `TITLE` property is the title of its BibTeX entry, and `org.bibtex.check` does not ask for one. (`org-bibtex-treat-headline-as-title`)
---@field org_bibtex_export_arbitrary_fields? boolean With `org_bibtex_prefix`, every property whose name has the prefix is a field of the entry exported (`org.bibtex.export`, `org.bibtex.copy`), not only those of its type. (`org-bibtex-export-arbitrary-fields`)
---@field org_bibtex_key_property? string The property that holds an entry's BibTeX key. `ID` makes the key a global ID. (`org-bibtex-key-property`)
---@field org_bibtex_tags? string[] Tags given to the entries the BibTeX commands make. (`org-bibtex-tags`)
---@field org_bibtex_tags_are_keywords? boolean The `keywords` field of an entry read is its tags (spaces as `_`, other characters a tag cannot have left out), and an entry's own tags are its `keywords` when exported, but for those of `org_bibtex_tags` and `org_bibtex_no_export_tags`. (`org-bibtex-tags-are-keywords`)
---@field org_bibtex_no_export_tags? string[] Tags that are not exported as keywords, with `org_bibtex_tags_are_keywords`. (`org-bibtex-no-export-tags`)
---@field org_bibtex_inherit_tags? boolean With `org_bibtex_tags_are_keywords`, the tags an entry inherits are keywords too. (`org-bibtex-inherit-tags`)
---@field org_bibtex_type_property_name? string The property that holds an entry's BibTeX type (`article`); a headline without it is no entry. (`org-bibtex-type-property-name`)
---@field org_link_abbrev_alist? table Link abbreviations: `[[gh:org-lsp]]` with `{ "gh": "https://github.com/%s" }` follows `https://github.com/org-lsp`. `%s` stands for what follows the colon, `%h` for it URL-encoded; without either it goes at the end. A file's `#+LINK:` lines come first. `%(my-function)` calls an Emacs function with what follows the colon (through Emacs), one `emacs_config` marks safe, as Org asks: `(put 'my-function 'org-link-abbrev-safe t)`. (`org-link-abbrev-alist`)
---@field org_file_apps? string[][] What opens a linked file, the first entry that matches: a match is an extension (`txt`), an Emacs regular expression for the file name (`\\.pdf\\'` in JSON; with groups `%1`, `%2`... in the command, it matches the file followed by the link's `::` part), `directory`, `remote`, `system` (what `C-u C-u C-c C-o` uses), `auto-mode` (every file the editor can show) or `t` (any other file). A command is `emacs` (the editor), `default` (the `system` entry, else the editor), `system` or `mailcap` (the system's application, through the editor), or a shell command where `%s` is the file. `C-u C-c C-o` opens the file in the editor. A Lisp function is not a command here. (`org-file-apps`)
---@field org_open_non_existing_files? boolean Open a linked file that is not there with the application `org_file_apps` gives it, rather than saying `No such file`. The editor opens such a file either way. (`org-open-non-existing-files`)
---@field org_open_directory_means_index_dot_org? boolean Following a link to a directory opens the `index.org` in it, rather than the directory. (`org-open-directory-means-index-dot-org`)
---@field org_tag_alist? string|string[] The tags offered when setting tags in a file without a `#+TAGS:` line, written like one: `"work(w) home(h) { @office(o) @home(m) } [ GTD : Control Persp ]"` gives `work` the key `w`, makes `@office` and `@home` exclusive, and groups `Control` and `Persp` under `GTD`; `\n` starts a new line. A list of these words does as well. Unset, the tags used in the file are offered. (`org-tag-alist`)
---@field org_tag_persistent_alist? string|string[] Tags offered in every file, before those of `#+TAGS:` or `org_tag_alist`, written the same way. (`org-tag-persistent-alist`)
---@field org_group_tags? boolean A group tag (`[ GTD : Control Persp ]`, `{ Context : @home @office }` in `#+TAGS:` or `org_tag_alist`) in the match string of a tags view stands for itself and the tags of its group. (`org-group-tags`)
---@field org_use_fast_todo_selection? 'auto'|'expert'|true|false How `C-c C-t` (`org.todo`) changes the TODO state where keywords have keys (`TODO(t)`): `auto` picks the state by key from a menu of them; `expert` by key too, the keys listed in the prompt alone (in Neovim and Vim; other editors show their menu); `false` goes to the next state, as where no keyword has a key. `true` is `auto`. (`org-use-fast-todo-selection`)
---@field org_use_fast_tag_selection? true|false|'auto' Pick tags by key, each key turning its tag on or off until RET: `auto` when some tag has a key, `true` always (keys are given to the tags without one), `false` never: the tags are typed instead. (`org-use-fast-tag-selection`)
---@field org_fast_tag_selection_single_key? boolean In fast tag selection, the first key that changes the tags also ends it. (`org-fast-tag-selection-single-key`)
---@field org_tags_sort_function? 'string<'|'string>' Sort the tags of a headline when they are set: alphabetically, or the other way. (`org-tags-sort-function`)
---@field org_auto_align_tags? boolean Align the tags of a headline to `org_tags_column` when they are set. (`org-auto-align-tags`)
---@field org_todo_repeat_to_state? string|true The state a repeating entry returns to when it is done: a keyword, or `true` for the state it had. (`org-todo-repeat-to-state`)
---@field org_priority_highest? string|integer The highest priority. (`org-priority-highest`)
---@field org_priority_lowest? string|integer The lowest priority. (`org-priority-lowest`)
---@field org_priority_default? string|integer The priority of an entry without a cookie. A file's `#+PRIORITIES:` line takes precedence over the three priority options. (`org-priority-default`)
---@field org_priority_start_cycle_with_default? boolean Raising or lowering the priority of an entry that has none starts at the default. (`org-priority-start-cycle-with-default`)
---@field org_time_stamp_rounding_minutes? integer The step in which the minutes of a timestamp change (the second number of Org's option). (`org-time-stamp-rounding-minutes`)
---@field org_read_date_prefer_future? true|false|'time' A date typed without a year or month is the next one to come. With `time`, a time that has passed today is tomorrow's. (`org-read-date-prefer-future`)
---@field org_agenda_jump_prefer_future? true|false|'time' How the agenda's `j` (`org.agenda.goto.date`) reads a date typed without a year or month, as `org_read_date_prefer_future` does for every other prompt. (`org-agenda-jump-prefer-future`)
---@field org_read_date_force_compatible_dates? boolean A typed year is kept within 1970 to 2037. (`org-read-date-force-compatible-dates`)
---@field org_refile_targets? org_lsp.RefileTarget[] Where entries can be refiled to. Each entry has `files`: `current` (the entry's own file), `agenda`, `org-id-files` (the files with IDs), a path or a list of paths; and one of `level`, `maxlevel`, `tag`, `todo`, `regexp` (the Rust `regex` crate's syntax) or `all` (true), which says which headlines there are targets. (`org-refile-targets`)
---@field org_refile_use_outline_path? true|false|'file'|'full-file-path'|'title' Name targets by their outline path (`Projects/Alpha/`). The three names start the path with the file, and offer the file itself, for its top level. (`org-refile-use-outline-path`)
---@field org_refile_active_region_within_subtree? boolean A selection that is not whole subtrees is refiled too: its first line becomes a headline first (`org-toggle-heading`, run by Emacs), a level below the entry it is in. (`org-refile-active-region-within-subtree`)
---@field org_refile_allow_creating_parent_nodes? true|false|'confirm' A target that is not there yet can be named after an existing one, `Projects/New`: it is made, as the last child of `Projects`, and the entry goes under it. `confirm` asks first. (`org-refile-allow-creating-parent-nodes`)
---@field org_refile_target_verify_function? string The name of an Emacs Lisp function that decides which headlines are refile targets: the server's Emacs calls it at the start of each, and keeps those it says yes to. Where it leaves the point, the search goes on: a function can skip a subtree. (`org-refile-target-verify-function`)
---@field org_reverse_note_order? boolean A refiled entry goes first under its target, not last. (`org-reverse-note-order`)
---@field org_archive_location? string Where archived subtrees go: a file, `::`, and a headline under which they go (they go to the end of the file without one). `%s` is the name of the file the subtree is in; an empty file part is that file. `datetree/` as the headline files them under year, month and day, by when they were closed. An `ARCHIVE` property of the entry or a parent, or a `#+ARCHIVE:` line, says otherwise. (`org-archive-location`)
---@field org_archive_reversed_order? boolean An archived subtree goes first under its archive headline, not last. (`org-archive-reversed-order`)
---@field org_archive_mark_done? boolean|string Mark archived entries done: `true` for the first done keyword, or the keyword to use. (`org-archive-mark-done`)
---@field org_archive_subtree_add_inherited_tags? true|false|'infile' Give an archived subtree the tags it inherited: always, never, or when it stays in its file. (`org-archive-subtree-add-inherited-tags`)
---@field org_archive_save_context_info? ('time'|'file'|'olpath'|'category'|'todo'|'itags'|'ltags'|'olid')[] What an archived entry keeps as `ARCHIVE_…` properties: `time`, `file`, `olpath` (its outline path), `category`, `todo`, `itags` (inherited tags), `ltags` (its own tags) and `olid` (its parent's ID). (`org-archive-save-context-info`)
---@field org_archive_sibling_heading? string The headline of the sibling `org.archive.sibling` archives under. (`org-archive-sibling-heading`)
---@field org_archive_file_header_format? string|false What a new archive file starts with; `%s` is the path of the file the subtree came from. (`org-archive-file-header-format`)
---@field org_archive_default_command? 'subtree'|'sibling'|'tag' What `org.archive.default` does: archive the subtree, archive it under its sibling, or tag it. (`org-archive-default-command`)
---@field org_archive_subtree_save_file_p? true|false|'from-org'|'from-agenda' Whether the archive file is saved once a subtree goes there: always (`true`), when archiving from an Org file (`from-org`), from the agenda (`from-agenda`), or never (`false`). Unsaved, it is left with its changes in the editor, as `auto_save` does not save it. (`org-archive-subtree-save-file-p`)
---@field org_capture_use_agenda_date? boolean Capturing from the agenda takes the day of the line the cursor is on, at midnight (or the hour of `org_extend_today_until`), for `%t`, `%U` and the like and for the date of a date tree, which a `time_prompt` template then does not ask for. (`org-capture-use-agenda-date`)
---@field org_capture_templates_contexts? table[] Where templates are offered. Each has the `key` of a template, `contexts`, rules such as `{ "in_file": "work" }` (`in_file`, `in_mode`, `in_buffer`, `not_in_file`, `not_in_mode` or `not_in_buffer`, each with a regexp of the `regex` crate's syntax, matched ignoring case), and maybe `use`, the key of the template it stands for there. A template with contexts is offered only where one of their rules holds. The mode is `org-mode` in an Org file and `org-agenda-mode` in the agenda, where there is no file; the buffer's name is the file's. (`org-capture-templates-contexts`)
---@field org_capture_templates? org_lsp.CaptureTemplate[] Capture templates. Each has a `key`, a `description`, a `type` (`entry`, the default, `plain`, `item`, `checkitem` or `table-line`), a `target` and a `template` (text, or a list of lines), and may have `prepend`, `empty_lines`, `empty_lines_before`, `empty_lines_after`, `immediate_finish`, `tree_type`, `time_prompt`, `table_line_pos`, `clock_in`, `clock_keep`, `clock_resume`, `jump_to_captured` and `unnarrowed`. The target is a list: `file` and a path; `file+headline`, a path and a headline; `file+olp`, a path and the headlines of an outline path; `file+regexp`, a path and a regular expression; `file+olp+datetree`, a path and the headlines of an outline path, maybe none, for a date tree under them; `id` and an ID; `clock`, the entry clocked now; `file+function`, a path and the name of an Emacs Lisp function that moves the point there, or `function` and the name of one that visits the file too (run by the server's Emacs). An empty path is `org_default_notes_file`, and without a target the entry goes at the end of `org_default_notes_file`. A template with only a key and a description groups the templates whose keys start with its key. (`org-capture-templates`)
---@field org_protocol_default_template_key? string The keys of the capture template an `org-protocol://capture` URL uses when it names none (`template=`). (`org-protocol-default-template-key`)
---@field org_protocol_data_separator? string An Emacs regular expression: what separates the parts of an old-style `org-protocol` URL (`org-protocol://capture:/x/URL/TITLE/BODY`). (`org-protocol-data-separator`)
---@field org_protocol_project_alist? table[] Where the files of `org-protocol://open-source` URLs are on this computer. Each has a `name`, the `base_url` of the pages (with its last slash) and the `working_directory` of their files (with its last slash, `~/` for the home directory), and may have an `online_suffix` taken off the URL (`.html`), a `working_suffix` put on in its place (`.org`), and `rewrites`, pairs of an Emacs regular expression on the URL, whose first group is taken out of it, and what goes after it, for a URL whose file is not there. The first whose `base_url` the URL has is tried first. (`org-protocol-project-alist`)
---@field org_protocol_editor? string The editor `org-lsp protocol` starts when no org-lsp runs, as a command line (`code`, `zed`, `kitty nvim`): it opens `org_default_notes_file` in it, or the file of an `open-source` URL, and the org-lsp that starts there takes the URL, within two minutes. Read from the shared file only. Without it, a stored link is kept for the next org-lsp that starts, and a capture fails.
---@field org_directory? string Where the relative paths of capture targets are. (`org-directory`)
---@field org_default_notes_file? string The target of a capture template whose file is empty. (`org-default-notes-file`)
---@field org_link_descriptive? boolean Show a link as its description (or its target, when it has none) and hide the rest: the brackets, and the target of a link with a description. The text is marked `hidden` in the highlighting, and editors that can conceal text conceal it. (`org-link-descriptive`)
---@field org_hide_emphasis_markers? boolean Hide the markers around `*bold*`, `/italic/`, `_underline_`, `+strike-through+`, `=verbatim=` and `~code~`, as for links. (`org-hide-emphasis-markers`)
---@field org_fontify_emphasized_text? boolean Show `*bold*`, `/italic/`, `_underline_`, `+strike-through+`, `=verbatim=` and `~code~` in their looks. With `false` they look as the text around them, their markers shown. (`org-fontify-emphasized-text`)
---@field org_emphasis_alist? table The look of each kind of emphasis, by its marker (`*`, `/`, `_`, `=`, `~`, `+`): a face of Org's, `bold`, `italic`, `bold-italic`, `underline`, `org-code` or `org-verbatim`, attributes as Org writes them (`:weight bold :slant italic :underline t :strike-through t`), or a list of them; `false` for none. As in Emacs, a marker left out has no look. Colors are not shown. (`org-emphasis-alist`)
---@field org_hidden_keywords? ('title'|'subtitle'|'author'|'email'|'date')[] The keywords hidden before their values, such as `#+TITLE:` with `["title"]`, in editors that conceal text. (`org-hidden-keywords`)
---@field org_hide_macro_markers? boolean Hide the braces of macro calls, `{{{` and `}}}`, in editors that conceal text. (`org-hide-macro-markers`)
---@field org_display_custom_times? boolean Show timestamps in `org_timestamp_custom_formats`: the date and time inside the brackets is hidden and the custom form shows in its place, as entities do. `#+STARTUP: customtime` turns it on for a file, and `C-c C-x C-t` (`org.timestamp.overlays.toggle`) on and off. Exports through Emacs use them too; the date prompt shows its answer in Org's own form. (`org-display-custom-times`)
---@field org_timestamp_custom_formats? string[] The formats of `org_display_custom_times`, as `format-time-string` reads them (`%d.%m.%Y`); brackets around a format are dropped. (`org-timestamp-custom-formats`)
---@field org_entities_user? any[][] Entities of your own, used as `\name`: what each is in LaTeX (and whether that needs math mode), HTML, ASCII, Latin-1 and UTF-8, as `["myarrow", "\\to", true, "&rarr;", "->", "->", "→"]`. A name is letters only, and one of Org's own is replaced. The server shows them as `org_pretty_entities` says and completes them; Emacs exports them. (`org-entities-user`)
---@field org_pretty_entities? boolean Show entities (`\alpha`, `\to`) as their character (`α`, `→`): the entity is hidden as link brackets are, and its character is an inlay hint in its place. (`org-pretty-entities`)
---@field org_pretty_entities_include_sub_superscripts? boolean With `org_pretty_entities`, show sub- and superscripts (`x^2`, `H_{2}O`, `a_{n}`) raised and lowered: in Unicode (`x²`, `H₂O`, `aₙ`) where every character has a raised or lowered form, as an inlay hint in place of the hidden text; the others are left as they are. (`org-pretty-entities-include-sub-superscripts`)
---@field org_use_sub_superscripts? true|false|'{}' Which sub- and superscripts `org_pretty_entities` shows: all, only those in braces (`a_{b}`, `"{}"`), or none. (`org-use-sub-superscripts`)
---@field return_uses_meta_return? boolean RET on the first line of a list item makes the next item, numbered as M-RET (`org-meta-return`) makes it (without a checkbox, which M-S-RET adds), the items after it renumbered, as nvim-orgmode's `org_return_uses_meta_return` does. Off, RET is Org's: a new line in the item. `org.return.item` (`<S-CR>`, Shift+Enter, with the vim keys) always does it.
---@field org_return_follows_link? boolean RET (`org.return`) on a link, a timestamp or a citation follows it, as `org.open` (`C-c C-o`) does. In a table RET still moves down. (`org-return-follows-link`)
---@field org_goto_max_level? integer The deepest headings `org.goto` (`C-c C-j`) offers. (`org-goto-max-level`)
---@field calendar_months? 1|3 How many months the calendar of the date prompt shows: 3 side by side, as Emacs's calendar shows them, the one with the selected day in the middle; or 1, that month alone, for a narrow window, with the answer on a line under the prompt.
---@field calendar_week_start_day? integer The day the weeks of the date prompt's calendar start on: 1 starts them on Monday. (`calendar-week-start-day`)
---@field emacs? string The Emacs program, for what only Org itself does: export (`org.export.dispatch`). The server starts it (`emacs --batch -Q`) when that is first asked for and keeps it running, so that Org loads once. Without Emacs, everything else works as before.
---@field emacs_config_file? string A file of Emacs Lisp that Emacs loads before Org, for the variables export reads (`org-export-backends`, `org-html-…`, `org-latex-…`, `org-publish-project-alist`): a file of its own, or your init file. `~/` is the home directory.
---@field emacs_config? string Emacs Lisp that Emacs evaluates after `emacs_config_file` and before loading Org, for a few settings without a file that `emacs_options` cannot say: a function or a hook, or `(add-to-list 'load-path "~/src/org-mode/lisp")` for another Org. A yes-or-no question Emacs asks while it works (`org-confirm-babel-evaluate`) is asked in the editor.
---@field emacs_options? table Org's options for what Emacs does, by their names in Org, which Emacs sets as Customize does: `{ "org-attach-method": "mv", "org-export-with-toc": false }`. `true` is `t` and `false` `nil`, a list is a list and a table an association list; a string is a symbol where the option takes one, or a function, by its name. Each is set when Emacs loads the library it is in, after `emacs_config_file` and `emacs_config`, and so wins over them. A value the option does not take is left out, with a message naming the part that does not fit. The editor's go over the shared file's option by option. The options, each with Org's documentation of it, are in `docs/emacs-options.org`; the others are settings of the server, or Lisp (hooks, functions written out), which goes in `emacs_config`.
---@field auto_save? boolean Save the files a command changes other than the one it was run in: an entry's file changed from the agenda, the target of a capture or of a refile. The editor saves them once it has made the change. Off, they are left with unsaved changes, as Emacs leaves them.
---@field mcp_edits? boolean Let the assistants `org-lsp mcp` serves change files: its tools `set_todo_state`, `capture`, `refile`, `schedule`, `deadline`, `set_tags`, `set_property`, `delete_property`, `set_priority`, `set_effort`, `add_note`, `archive` and `delete_entry`, which write the agenda files, the capture targets, the files of `org_refile_targets` and the archive files directly. Off, it only reads. It is read from the shared configuration file, as everything `org-lsp mcp` reads.
---@field appt_message_warning_time? integer|integer[] How many minutes before an appointment (an entry of today's agenda with a time of day) its reminders start: one then, and one each `appt_display_interval` minutes after, as Emacs's `appt.el` reminds; an entry's `APPT_WARNTIME` property says it for that entry. As org-lsp's own, a list gives the minutes before the appointment of each reminder: `[10, 0]` reminds ten minutes before and when it is due. `org-lsp notify` reminds this way; through Emacs (Add today's appointments from the agenda files), a list stands for its largest number. (`appt-message-warning-time`)
---@field appt_display_interval? integer Minutes between two reminders of an appointment, from the first, `appt_message_warning_time` minutes before it. (`appt-display-interval`)
---@field notification_use_timestamp_warning? boolean As org-lsp's own, `org-lsp notify` also reminds of an appointment from a deadline or a timestamp as many hours before it as the timestamp's warning cookie says: once, 60 minutes before `<2026-10-22 Thu 21:00 -1h>`, besides the reminders of `appt_message_warning_time`. A scheduled date's cookie is a delay and does not count, nor do cookies in days, weeks, months or years. Emacs's `appt.el` reads no cookie.
---@field notification_deadline_summary_time? string The time of day, as `HH:MM`, at which `org-lsp notify` lists the deadlines the day's agenda shows that are not done: those of the day, those whose warning period has begun, and those past.
---@field notification_match? string The entries `org-lsp notify` reminds of: a match string of the tags view, such as `+meeting|+call` or `-private`.
---@field notification_command? string[] A program `org-lsp notify` runs for each notification, and its arguments, in which `%t` stands for the title, `%b` for the text and `%%` for `%`: `["ntfy", "publish", "--title", "%t", "mytopic", "%b"]`. It runs instead of the desktop notification, unless `notification_desktop` is on. No shell reads it. With a placeholder of an entry (those of `notification_title_format`), it runs once for each appointment, with that appointment's title, text and values.
---@field notification_title_format? string The title of an appointment's notification from `org-lsp notify`, in which `%h` stands for the headline, `%k` its TODO keyword, `%p` its priority, `%T` its tags, `%c` its category, `%s` and `%d` its scheduled and deadline timestamps, `%w` the appointment's time, `%m` the minutes left, `%e` the effort, `%f` and `%F` the file's path and name, `%l` the headline's line, `%a` the line `appt.el` shows and `%%` `%`: `"%k %h"`. Appointments due together share the first one's.
---@field notification_body_format? string The text of an appointment's notification from `org-lsp notify`, with the placeholders of `notification_title_format`: `"%w %c %T"`. Appointments due together have a line each.
---@field notification_deadline_line_format? string A line of the deadline summary of `org-lsp notify`, with the placeholders of `notification_title_format` and `%D` for the agenda's leader (`In 3 d.:`, `2 d. ago:`, `Deadline:`); there `%a` is the headline as the agenda shows it.
---@field notification_desktop? boolean Whether `org-lsp notify` shows a desktop notification: with `notify-send` on Linux and the other Unix systems, `terminal-notifier` on macOS when it is installed and `osascript` otherwise, PowerShell on Windows (BurntToast if it is installed).
---@field notification_icon? string The image of the desktop notifications of `org-lsp notify`: the path of a PNG file (`~/` for the home directory), or `""` for none. Unset, the logo, which it writes to `org-lsp/icon.png` in the user's data directory (`~/.local/share` on Linux). `osascript` shows no image: on macOS it takes `terminal-notifier`.
---@field org_cycle_separator_lines? integer With at least this many blank lines after a subtree, the last one stays outside its fold. Negative: all of them stay outside. `0`: none does. (`org-cycle-separator-lines`)
---@field org_cycle_include_plain_lists? true|false|'integrate' Whether list items fold, as subtrees do. `integrate` makes them part of the outline: TAB showing a headline's children folds its lists to their top items too. `false`: they do not fold. (`org-cycle-include-plain-lists`)
---@field org_cycle_max_level? integer The deepest headlines that fold; deeper ones are text of the subtree they are in. (`org-cycle-max-level`)
---@field org_cycle_global_at_bob? boolean TAB at the very start of the file, when its first line is no headline, cycles the whole file as S-TAB does. (`org-cycle-global-at-bob`)
---@field org_cycle_level_after_item_entry_creation? boolean TAB on a new, empty headline makes it a child of the headline before, then each level up, then a sibling again; on an empty item, it indents and outdents the item the same way. (`org-cycle-level-after-item/entry-creation`)
---@field org_startup_folded? 'overview'|'fold'|'content'|'show2levels'|'show3levels'|'show4levels'|'show5levels'|'showeverything'|'nofold'|'showall' How files show when opened: `"overview"` (or `"fold"`) only the top headlines, `"content"` all the headlines, `"show2levels"` to `"show5levels"` the headlines down to that level, `"showeverything"` (or `"nofold"`, `"showall"`) everything. `#+STARTUP:` decides for a file. Unless everything shows, a subtree's `VISIBILITY` property (`folded`, `children`, `content`, `all`) decides for it, archived subtrees are folded, and drawers too. Folds hide whole subtrees, so where Org shows a headline's children but not its text, the text shows. (`org-startup-folded`)
---@field org_startup_align_all_tables? boolean Align every table of a file when it opens (an edit of the file, as in Emacs). `#+STARTUP: align` and `noalign` decide for a file. (`org-startup-align-all-tables`)
---@field org_startup_shrink_all_tables? boolean Shrink the columns of every table that have a width cookie (`<10>`) when a file opens. `#+STARTUP: shrink` decides for a file. Shrunk fields show their first characters and `…`, in editors that can hide text. (`org-startup-shrink-all-tables`)
---@field org_cycle_hide_block_startup? boolean Fold blocks when a file opens folded (`org_startup_folded`). `#+STARTUP: hideblocks` and `nohideblocks` decide for a file. (`org-cycle-hide-block-startup`)
---@field org_cycle_hide_drawer_startup? boolean Fold drawers when a file opens folded (`org_startup_folded`). (`org-cycle-hide-drawer-startup`)
---@field org_cycle_open_archived_trees? boolean Leave archived subtrees open when a file opens folded (`org_startup_folded`). (`org-cycle-open-archived-trees`)
---@field org_adapt_indentation? true|false|'headline-data' Indent the lines of an entry past its headline's stars: `true` for all of them, `"headline-data"` for the planning line, drawers and clock lines right below the headline. Applies to new lines, to re-indenting (range formatting) and to what commands write. (`org-adapt-indentation`)
---@field org_image_actual_width? boolean|number|number[] How wide the image of a link shows: `true` at its own width; a number, that many pixels; `false` or a list of one number, the `:width` of the paragraph's `#+ATTR_ORG:` or else of another `#+ATTR_…:` (pixels, `50%` or a fraction up to 2 of the text's width, `t` for its own width), else the list's number. An entry's `ORG-IMAGE-ACTUAL-WIDTH` property decides for its own text. (`org-image-actual-width`)
---@field org_image_max_width? 'fill-column'|'window'|null|number How wide at most an image shown at its own width is: `"fill-column"`, 70 columns; `"window"`, the window's width; an integer, that many pixels; a float, that part of the window's width; null for no limit. (`org-image-max-width`)
---@field org_display_remote_inline_images? 'skip'|'download'|'cache' Whether the images of `http:` and `https:` links show (`C-c C-x C-v`): `"skip"` not; `"download"` downloaded each time they show; `"cache"` downloaded once and kept for the session. The download follows `org_resource_download_policy`; an image larger than 20 MB is not downloaded. (`org-display-remote-inline-images`)
---@field org_resource_download_policy? true|'prompt'|'safe'|false Which remote resources are downloaded (the images of links, and in Emacs a file attached from a URL, `C-c C-a u`, or a remote `#+INCLUDE:` or `#+SETUPFILE:` in an export): `true` all of them; `"prompt"` those considered safe, and the others when the answer to Emacs's question says so (in Emacs, a yes or no: yes downloads it this once); `"safe"` only those considered safe; `false` none but those. (`org-resource-download-policy`)
---@field org_safe_remote_resources? string[] The remote resources considered safe: Emacs regexps matched against a resource's URL and against the `file://` URL of the file asking for it. The answers `!`, `d` and `f` to the download question add one for the rest of the session. Emacs has them too, for what it downloads. (`org-safe-remote-resources`)
---@field org_startup_with_link_previews? boolean Show the images of links to image files in place when a file opens, as `C-c C-x C-v` (`org.link.preview`) shows them. `#+STARTUP: inlineimages` (or `linkpreviews`) and `noinlineimages` decide for a file. Editors that can show images: Neovim from 0.13 (`vim.ui.img`), in a terminal with the Kitty graphics protocol. (`org-startup-with-link-previews`)
---@field org_startup_with_latex_preview? boolean Show the LaTeX fragments as images when a file opens, as `C-c C-x C-l` (`org.latex.preview`) shows them, made by Emacs. `#+STARTUP: latexpreview` and `nolatexpreview` decide for a file. (`org-startup-with-latex-preview`)
---@field org_startup_indented? boolean Show files indented by level without changing them (`org-indent-mode`): the text under a headline lines up with its title, and leading stars are hidden. `#+STARTUP: indent` and `noindent` decide for a file. It turns `org_adapt_indentation` off in such files, unless that is `"headline-data"`. The lines a long line wraps into go on under its text, a headline's title or an item's text (`wrap-prefix`) in Neovim and Vim, without the prefix; under the prefix in Zed and Helix. (`org-startup-indented`)
---@field org_startup_numerated? boolean Number the headlines of files (`org-num-mode`): `1`, `1.1`, `1.2`… after the stars, shown as inlay hints without changing the file. `#+STARTUP: num` and `nonum` decide for a file, and `org.num.mode` turns it on and off. (`org-startup-numerated`)
---@field org_num_max_level? integer The deepest level `org-num-mode` numbers. (`org-num-max-level`)
---@field org_num_skip_commented? boolean `org-num-mode` leaves `COMMENT` subtrees unnumbered. (`org-num-skip-commented`)
---@field org_num_skip_footnotes? boolean `org-num-mode` leaves the footnotes section (`* Footnotes`) unnumbered. (`org-num-skip-footnotes`)
---@field org_num_skip_tags? string[] `org-num-mode` leaves the subtrees of headlines with one of these tags (their own, not inherited) unnumbered. (`org-num-skip-tags`)
---@field org_num_skip_unnumbered? boolean `org-num-mode` leaves the subtrees of headlines with an `UNNUMBERED` property unnumbered. (`org-num-skip-unnumbered`)
---@field org_hide_leading_stars? boolean Hide all the stars of a headline but the last; they keep their place. `#+STARTUP: hidestars` and `showstars` decide for a file. (`org-hide-leading-stars`)
---@field org_indent_mode_turns_on_hiding_stars? boolean Where `org_startup_indented` (or `#+STARTUP: indent`) shows a file indented, hide the leading stars too, whatever `org_hide_leading_stars` says. (`org-indent-mode-turns-on-hiding-stars`)
---@field org_indent_mode_turns_off_org_adapt_indentation? boolean Where a file shows indented, `org_adapt_indentation` is off (unless it is `headline-data`): new and moved text is not indented to its headline. (`org-indent-mode-turns-off-org-adapt-indentation`)
---@field org_indent_indentation_per_level? integer How many columns a file shown indented moves each level of headlines and their text in; 0 shows them as they are. (`org-indent-indentation-per-level`)
---@field org_duration_format? 'h:mm'|'h:mm:ss'|('compact'|any[])[] How durations are written (clock tables, clock lines, efforts): `"h:mm"` or `"h:mm:ss"`, or a list of units each with whether it is written when zero (`["d", false]`), with perhaps `["special", "h:mm"]` (the hours and minutes as `H:MM` after the larger units), `["special", "h:mm:ss"]`, `["special", 2]` (one unit with 2 decimals) and `"compact"` (no space between units). The units are those of `org_duration_units`. (`org-duration-format`)
---@field org_duration_units? any[][] The units of durations and the minutes in each, for reading efforts and writing durations: `["d", 480]` for days of 8 hours. (`org-duration-units`)
---@field org_fontify_done_headline? boolean Show a headline marked done in the look of done headlines past its keyword (`org-headline-done`). (`org-fontify-done-headline`)
---@field org_fontify_todo_headline? boolean Show a headline marked with a keyword not done in the look of TODO headlines past its keyword (`org-headline-todo`, the token modifier `todo`). (`org-fontify-todo-headline`)
---@field org_fontify_quote_and_verse_blocks? boolean Show the lines inside quote and verse blocks in their own look (`org-quote`, `org-verse`: the token types `quote` and `verse`). (`org-fontify-quote-and-verse-blocks`)
---@field org_custom_properties? string[] The properties `org.custom.properties.toggle` (`org-toggle-custom-properties-visibility`) hides in the property drawers, and shows again: `["ID", "CUSTOM_ID"]`. Editors that can hide them: Neovim from 0.11, and Sublime Text, in a fold. (`org-custom-properties`)
---@field org_n_level_faces? integer How many looks headlines take by level, from 1 to 8 (the token modifiers `level1` to `level8`, Org's `org-level-1` to `org-level-8`): with 4, a headline of level 5 looks as one of level 1, or as one of level 4 with `org_cycle_level_faces` off. (`org-n-level-faces`)
---@field org_cycle_level_faces? boolean Past the last look of `org_n_level_faces`, headlines take the looks of the levels from the first again; with `false`, those deeper all keep the last. (`org-cycle-level-faces`)
---@field org_fontify_whole_heading_line? boolean Show the look of a headline's level past the end of its text, to the edge of the window: for looks with a background. Editors that can show it: Neovim, Vim and VS Code (`org-lsp/lineHighlights`, VS Code's colors `org.headlineLevel1Background` and on). (`org-fontify-whole-heading-line`)
---@field org_ellipsis? string What a closed fold shows after its first line, such as `" ▾"`, in the highlight group `OrgLspEllipsis` (Neovim). Editors that can show it: Neovim and Vim. (`org-ellipsis`)
---@field org_bullets_mode? boolean org-bullets: the last star of each headline shows as a bullet, those of `org_bullets_bullet_list` by level, and the stars before it are hidden, as with `org_hide_leading_stars`. (`org-bullets-mode`)
---@field org_bullets_bullet_list? string[] The bullets of `org_bullets_mode`, one a level, repeating after the last. (`org-bullets-bullet-list`)
---@field org_bullets_list_bullet? false|string What `org_bullets_mode` shows in place of the `-`, `+` or `*` of an item, as org-bullets.nvim does; `false` leaves them as they are.
---@field org_bullets_checkboxes? false|table What `org_bullets_mode` shows between the brackets of a checkbox, `[ ]`, `[-]` and `[X]`, as org-bullets.nvim does; `false` leaves them as they are. A state left out keeps its default.
---@field org_src_lang_modes? table The major mode, without `-mode`, of a language of source blocks, over Org's own (`elisp` → `emacs-lisp`, `sh` → `sh`): `{ "ipython": "python" }`. Neovim and Vim color the code of a block in the language of its mode (`org-lsp/codeBlocks`), by their name for it (`emacs-lisp` → `elisp`, `sh` → `bash`); Emacs, where it edits or exports a block, takes it too. (`org-src-lang-modes`)
---@field org_src_preserve_indentation? boolean Leave the code of source blocks as it is indented when re-indenting. (`org-src-preserve-indentation`)
---@field org_babel_tangle_lang_exts? table By language, the extension of the file `:tangle yes` writes its blocks to, before Org's own (`emacs-lisp` → `el`): `{ "python": "py" }`. A language without one gives its name. (`org-babel-tangle-lang-exts`)
---@field org_babel_tangle_use_relative_file_links? boolean Whether the link comments of tangled files (`:comments link`) point back to the Org file by a relative path; with `false` by its whole path. (`org-babel-tangle-use-relative-file-links`)
---@field org_babel_tangle_comment_format_beg? string The comment before a tangled block (`:comments link`): `%link` the link back to it, `%source-name` its name, `%file` the Org file, `%start-line` the line its code starts on. (`org-babel-tangle-comment-format-beg`)
---@field org_babel_tangle_comment_format_end? string The comment after a tangled block, with the keys of `org_babel_tangle_comment_format_beg`. (`org-babel-tangle-comment-format-end`)
---@field org_babel_tangle_uncomment_comments? boolean Write the comments of tangled blocks as they are: not commented, and no line break after them. (`org-babel-tangle-uncomment-comments`)
---@field org_babel_process_comment_text? string The function the Org text of `:comments org` goes through: `org-remove-indentation` takes its common indentation away, `identity` leaves it; Emacs tangles with any other. (`org-babel-process-comment-text`)
---@field org_babel_tangle_default_file_mode? string In octal, the mode a symbolic `:tangle-mode` (`u+x`) changes. (`org-babel-tangle-default-file-mode`)
---@field org_babel_tangle_remove_file_before_write? true|false|'auto' Whether a file tangling writes again is deleted first: `true` always (a link to it becomes a file of its own), `false` never (a read-only file refuses), `"auto"` when it is read-only. (`org-babel-tangle-remove-file-before-write`)
---@field org_babel_noweb_wrap_start? string What opens a Noweb reference in a source block (`<<name>>`). (`org-babel-noweb-wrap-start`)
---@field org_babel_noweb_wrap_end? string What closes a Noweb reference. (`org-babel-noweb-wrap-end`)
---@field org_src_content_indentation? integer How far code is indented past its source block: when re-indenting, and when the code of a block edited in a file of its own (`C-c '`) is written back. Formerly `org_edit_src_content_indentation`, which is still read. (`org-src-content-indentation`)
---@field org_agenda_span? 'day'|'week'|'fortnight'|'month'|'year'|integer How much time the agenda shows. (`org-agenda-span`)
---@field org_agenda_start_on_weekday? integer|false The day a week or fortnight starts on; `false` starts it today. (`org-agenda-start-on-weekday`)
---@field org_deadline_warning_days? integer How many days before a deadline it shows up. (`org-deadline-warning-days`)
---@field org_scheduled_delay_days? integer How many days after its date a scheduled entry shows up. (`org-scheduled-delay-days`)
---@field org_scheduled_past_days? integer How many days a scheduled entry that is not done keeps showing up. (`org-scheduled-past-days`)
---@field org_modules? string[] Org modules to load. `org-habit` turns habits on: entries with the `STYLE` property `habit` and a scheduled date with a repeater (`.+2d`, `.+2d/4d` for a deadline too) show in the agenda with a graph of the days they were done. `ol-man` makes `[[man:ls]]` a link to a man page (`org_man_command`). `org-ctags` follows links to the tags of a `TAGS` file, as loading it and calling `org-ctags-enable` does (`org_ctags_open_link_functions`). Others are ignored. (`org-modules`)
---@field org_habit_graph_column? integer The column the consistency graph of a habit starts at in the agenda. (`org-habit-graph-column`)
---@field org_habit_preceding_days? integer How many days before today a habit's graph shows. (`org-habit-preceding-days`)
---@field org_habit_following_days? integer How many days after today a habit's graph shows. (`org-habit-following-days`)
---@field org_habit_show_habits? boolean Show habits in the agenda. (`org-habit-show-habits`)
---@field org_habit_show_habits_only_for_today? boolean Show habits only on today's date, not on future days of the agenda. (`org-habit-show-habits-only-for-today`)
---@field org_habit_show_all_today? boolean Show every habit today, even those not scheduled for today. (`org-habit-show-all-today`)
---@field org_habit_today_glyph? string The mark of today in a habit's graph. (`org-habit-today-glyph`)
---@field org_habit_completed_glyph? string The mark of a day a habit was done in its graph. (`org-habit-completed-glyph`)
---@field org_habit_show_done_always_green? boolean A day a habit was done is green in its graph even when it was done late. (`org-habit-show-done-always-green`)
---@field org_habit_scheduled_past_days? integer How many days a habit that is not done keeps showing up. (`org-habit-scheduled-past-days`)
---@field org_deadline_past_days? integer How many days a missed deadline keeps showing up. (`org-deadline-past-days`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_scheduled_if_done? boolean Leave out scheduled entries that are done. (`org-agenda-skip-scheduled-if-done`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_deadline_if_done? boolean Leave out deadlines of entries that are done. (`org-agenda-skip-deadline-if-done`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_timestamp_if_done? boolean Leave out timestamps of entries that are done. (`org-agenda-skip-timestamp-if-done`)
---@field org_agenda_time_leading_zero? boolean Write times with two digits for the hour (`08:00`). (`org-agenda-time-leading-zero`)
---@field org_agenda_timegrid_use_ampm? boolean Write the times of the agenda, the time grid's too, in 12-hour form (`8:00am`). (`org-agenda-timegrid-use-ampm`)
---@field org_agenda_search_headline_for_time? boolean Take a time in a headline (`Call at 15:00`) as the time of its entry. (`org-agenda-search-headline-for-time`)
---@field org_agenda_remove_times_when_in_prefix? true|false|'beg' Take a time out of the headline when the prefix shows it; `beg` only one at the start of the headline. (`org-agenda-remove-times-when-in-prefix`)
---@field org_agenda_default_appointment_duration? integer Minutes an entry with a start time and no end time lasts: the agenda shows it as a range. (`org-agenda-default-appointment-duration`)
---@field org_agenda_remove_timeranges_from_blocks? boolean Take a date range out of the headline of its lines. (`org-agenda-remove-timeranges-from-blocks`)
---@field org_agenda_diary_sexp_prefix? string A regexp (Emacs's syntax): what it matches of a diary sexp's text goes in front of the line, as a scheduled date's `Scheduled:` does. (`org-agenda-diary-sexp-prefix`)
---@field org_agenda_show_inherited_tags? boolean|'always'|('agenda'|'todo'|'search')[] Show the inherited tags of an entry on its agenda lines: where `org_agenda_use_tag_inheritance` has them (`true`), always, never (`false`), or in these kinds of views (`agenda`, `todo`, `search`). Sorting by tag and `org_agenda_max_tags` go by the tags a line shows. (`org-agenda-show-inherited-tags`)
---@field org_agenda_use_tag_inheritance? boolean|('agenda'|'todo'|'search')[] The kinds of views whose lines have inherited tags (`true`: all). Tags matches always have them. (`org-agenda-use-tag-inheritance`)
---@field org_agenda_hide_tags_regexp? string A regexp (Emacs's syntax): the tags it matches are not shown on agenda lines, but filters still see them. (`org-agenda-hide-tags-regexp`)
---@field org_agenda_include_deadlines? boolean Show deadlines in the agenda. (`org-agenda-include-deadlines`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_additional_timestamps_same_entry? boolean Once a timestamp of an entry has made a line for a day, leave out its other timestamps on that day. (`org-agenda-skip-additional-timestamps-same-entry`)
---@field org_agenda_fontify_priorities? true|false|'cookies' Highlight the priority cookies of agenda lines (`cookies`), or each line from its cookie on (`true`; links and tags keep their own look); the highest priority shows in bold, the lowest in italic. `false` highlights none. Org's list of faces by priority is not read here: `org_priority_faces` gives them. (`org-agenda-fontify-priorities`)
---@field org_agenda_prefer_last_repeat? boolean|string[] Show a repeating scheduled date, deadline or timestamp at its last repeat up to today instead of its base date, for every entry (`true`) or for those with one of these TODO keywords. (`org-agenda-prefer-last-repeat`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_deadline_prewarning_if_scheduled? boolean|'pre-scheduled'|integer For a scheduled entry, show its deadline only on its date (`true`), not before its scheduled date (`pre-scheduled`), or from N days before it (a number). (`org-agenda-skip-deadline-prewarning-if-scheduled`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_scheduled_delay_if_deadline? boolean|'post-deadline'|integer For an entry with a deadline, drop the delay of its scheduled date (`--2d`) (`true`), or keep it no later than the deadline (`post-deadline`). A number drops it too, as Org's code does. (`org-agenda-skip-scheduled-delay-if-deadline`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_scheduled_if_deadline_is_shown? boolean|'not-today'|'repeated-after-deadline' Leave out a scheduled date on a day its entry's deadline shows (`true`), only when it was scheduled before today (`not-today`); `repeated-after-deadline` is `org_agenda_skip_scheduled_repeats_after_deadline`. Not for habits. (`org-agenda-skip-scheduled-if-deadline-is-shown`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_scheduled_repeats_after_deadline? boolean Leave out the repeats of a scheduled date that come after the entry's deadline. (`org-agenda-skip-scheduled-repeats-after-deadline`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_comment_trees? boolean Leave out of every view the entries of a commented subtree (`COMMENT` after the stars). (`org-agenda-skip-comment-trees`)
---@field org_agenda_skip_archived_trees? boolean Leave out of every view the entries of an archived subtree (tagged `ARCHIVE`), unless archives mode (`v a`) shows them. Org advises leaving it on and using `v a` instead. (`org-agenda-skip-archived-trees`)
---@field org_agenda_todo_list_sublevels? boolean Show in the TODO list the TODO entries below another one. Off, the list skips the subtree of each entry it shows or leaves out for a date. (`org-agenda-todo-list-sublevels`)
---@field org_agenda_todo_ignore_with_date? boolean Leave out of the TODO list the entries with an active timestamp anywhere in their text, scheduled and deadline dates too. (`org-agenda-todo-ignore-with-date`)
---@field org_agenda_todo_ignore_scheduled? boolean|'future'|'past'|'all'|integer Leave out of the TODO list the scheduled entries: all of them (`true`, `all`), those scheduled after today (`future`), today or before (`past`), or with a number N, N days from now or later (a negative N: N days ago or before). (`org-agenda-todo-ignore-scheduled`)
---@field org_agenda_todo_ignore_deadlines? boolean|'near'|'far'|'all'|'future'|'past'|integer Leave out of the TODO list the entries with a deadline: in its warning period, that is, shown in the agenda (`true`, `near`), not yet in it (`far`), all of them (`all`), after today (`future`), today or before (`past`), or with a number as for `org_agenda_todo_ignore_scheduled`. (`org-agenda-todo-ignore-deadlines`)
---@field org_agenda_todo_ignore_timestamp? boolean|'future'|'past'|'all'|integer Leave out of the TODO list the entries with an active timestamp that is no scheduled or deadline date, with the values of `org_agenda_todo_ignore_scheduled`. (`org-agenda-todo-ignore-timestamp`)
---@field org_agenda_todo_ignore_time_comparison_use_seconds? boolean Compare the dates of the `org_agenda_todo_ignore_*` settings with now to the second instead of by day: `future` is then later than now, and a number counts seconds. (`org-agenda-todo-ignore-time-comparison-use-seconds`)
---@field org_tags_match_list_sublevels? true|false|'indented' Whether tags matches (agenda, sparse trees) and stuck projects list the entries below one they list: `false` passes over its subtree, `indented` shows them with a dot for each level below the first. (`org-tags-match-list-sublevels`)
---@field org_agenda_tags_todo_honor_ignore_options? boolean Have the `org_agenda_todo_ignore_*` settings leave entries out of `tags-todo` matches as well. (`org-agenda-tags-todo-honor-ignore-options`)
---@field org_agenda_sorting_strategy? ('time-up'|'time-down'|'timestamp-up'|'timestamp-down'|'scheduled-up'|'scheduled-down'|'deadline-up'|'deadline-down'|'tsia-up'|'tsia-down'|'ts-up'|'ts-down'|'stats-up'|'stats-down'|'priority-up'|'priority-down'|'urgency-up'|'urgency-down'|'effort-up'|'effort-down'|'category-up'|'category-down'|'category-keep'|'tag-up'|'tag-down'|'todo-state-up'|'todo-state-down'|'habit-up'|'habit-down'|'alpha-up'|'alpha-down'|'user-defined-up'|'user-defined-down')[]|table How the lines of a day (`agenda`), the TODO list (`todo`), a tags match (`tags`, `tags-todo` too) and a search (`search`) are ordered: the strategies in turn, each deciding when the ones before do not. A view without a list takes `agenda`'s, and without that `time-up`, `category-keep`, `urgency-down`; one list alone is for every view. `time-up`/`-down` the time of day; `timestamp-`, `scheduled-`, `deadline-`, `ts-` (active) and `tsia-` (inactive) `up`/`down` the date of that kind (in the TODO list and a tags match, the entry's); `priority-` the priority, `urgency-` the priority with how near or overdue the date is; `effort-`, `category-` (and `category-keep`, the order the entries come in), `tag-` (the last tag), `todo-state-` (where the keyword is in the file's list, done ones first), `habit-`, `alpha-` (the headline), `stats-` (the statistics cookie). `user-defined-up` and `-down` call Lisp, which the server does not run. (`org-agenda-sorting-strategy`)
---@field org_agenda_sort_notime_is_late? boolean Lines without a time (or without the date a strategy sorts by) come after those with one. (`org-agenda-sort-notime-is-late`)
---@field org_agenda_sort_noeffort_is_high? boolean Entries without an effort count as having the highest when sorted, filtered or limited by effort. (`org-agenda-sort-noeffort-is-high`)
---@field org_agenda_max_entries? integer|table How many entries a day of the agenda, the TODO list, a tags match or a search shows at most: one number for all, or one for each kind of view. Other lines, such as those of the time grid, go as well; with a negative number they stay. (`org-agenda-max-entries`)
---@field org_agenda_max_todos? integer|table How many entries with a TODO keyword a view shows at most, the other lines going as well; with a negative number, those stay. (`org-agenda-max-todos`)
---@field org_agenda_max_tags? integer|table How many entries with tags a view shows at most, the other lines going as well; with a negative number, those stay. (`org-agenda-max-tags`)
---@field org_agenda_max_effort? integer|table The most minutes of effort the entries of a view add up to: entries show while their efforts, in order, stay within it (an entry without one counts as the highest, or as -1 with `org_agenda_sort_noeffort_is_high` off). (`org-agenda-max-effort`)
---@field org_agenda_show_future_repeats? true|false|'next' Show the future dates of repeating entries: all of them, none, or only the next. (`org-agenda-show-future-repeats`)
---@field org_agenda_show_all_dates? boolean Show days without entries. (`org-agenda-show-all-dates`)
---@field org_agenda_tags_column? integer|'auto' Where tags are aligned in the agenda: a column (negative: where they end), or `auto` for the right edge of the window. With `auto`, the editors that tell the server how wide the agenda's window is (Neovim, Vim, Sublime Text) have the tags there, and the agenda follows the window as it is resized; the others (VS Code, Zed) have them end at column 80. (`org-agenda-tags-column`)
---@field org_agenda_use_time_grid? boolean Draw the time grid. (`org-agenda-use-time-grid`)
---@field org_agenda_time_grid? any[] The time grid, as Org writes it: the days that have it (`daily`: a one-day agenda; `weekly`: every day of a longer one; `today`: today; `require-timed`: only days with an entry that has a time; `remove-match`: no grid line at a time an entry already has), the times of its lines (`800` for 8:00), what follows a time that has no end time, and the text of a grid line. (`org-agenda-time-grid`)
---@field org_agenda_current_time_string? string The text of the line for the current time. (`org-agenda-current-time-string`)
---@field org_agenda_show_current_time_in_grid? boolean Show the current time in the grid. (`org-agenda-show-current-time-in-grid`)
---@field org_agenda_weekend_days? integer[] The days drawn as the weekend (0 is Sunday). (`org-agenda-weekend-days`)
---@field org_agenda_start_with_log_mode? boolean Start the agenda in log mode, which shows what was closed and clocked each day (`l` toggles it). (`org-agenda-start-with-log-mode`)
---@field org_agenda_start_with_clockreport_mode? boolean Start the agenda with the clock report under it (`R` toggles it). (`org-agenda-start-with-clockreport-mode`)
---@field org_agenda_clockreport_parameter_plist? string The parameters of the clock report's table, as those of a clock table block (`:maxlevel 3 :tags t`); `:block`, `:tstart`, `:tend` and `:scope` are the agenda's. (`org-agenda-clockreport-parameter-plist`)
---@field org_agenda_clock_report_header? string Text on a line of its own (or lines) before the clock report. (`org-agenda-clock-report-header`)
---@field org_agenda_start_with_archives_mode? 'trees'|'files'|false Start the agenda with entries in archived trees (`trees`; `v a` toggles it), or those and the files the agenda files archive to (`files`; `v A`). (`org-agenda-start-with-archives-mode`)
---@field org_agenda_log_mode_items? ('closed'|'clock'|'state')[] What log mode shows: `closed` entries, `clock` lines and `state` changes. (`org-agenda-log-mode-items`)
---@field org_agenda_log_mode_add_notes? boolean Show the note of a state change or a clock line after the headline in log mode. (`org-agenda-log-mode-add-notes`)
---@field org_agenda_move_date_from_past_immediately_to_today? boolean Moving a date of the past one day later from the agenda (`S-<right>`) takes it to today. (`org-agenda-move-date-from-past-immediately-to-today`)
---@field org_agenda_persistent_marks? boolean Keep the marks of a bulk action (`B`) after it (`p` in its menu toggles this for one action). (`org-agenda-persistent-marks`)
---@field org_agenda_show_outline_path? true|false|'title' As the cursor moves in the agenda, show where the entry of its line is: the file's name and the headlines above it (`work.org/Projects/Report`), in the echo area or the status bar. With `title` the file's `#+TITLE:` comes first instead of its name. (`org-agenda-show-outline-path`)
---@field org_agenda_start_with_follow_mode? boolean Start the agenda in follow mode, which shows the entry of the cursor's line in another window as the cursor moves (`F` toggles it). (`org-agenda-start-with-follow-mode`)
---@field org_agenda_start_with_entry_text_mode? boolean Start the agenda with some text of each entry under its line (`E` toggles it). (`org-agenda-start-with-entry-text-mode`)
---@field org_agenda_entry_text_maxlines? integer The most lines of an entry's text `E` shows. (`org-agenda-entry-text-maxlines`)
---@field org_agenda_entry_text_leaders? string What each line of an entry's text starts with in the agenda. (`org-agenda-entry-text-leaders`)
---@field org_agenda_entry_text_exclude_regexps? string[] Emacs regexps whose matches are left out of the entry text `E` shows. (`org-agenda-entry-text-exclude-regexps`)
---@field org_agenda_add_entry_text_maxlines? integer The most lines of each entry's text written under its line when the agenda is written to a plain text file (`C-x C-w`), each after four spaces and `>`; 0 writes none. The text `E` shows is not written. (`org-agenda-add-entry-text-maxlines`)
---@field org_agenda_add_entry_text_descriptive_links? boolean Whether links in the text `org_agenda_add_entry_text_maxlines` writes show as their descriptions. In Org 9.8.10 a plain text file has the links as they are either way, and so does org-lsp. (`org-agenda-add-entry-text-descriptive-links`)
---@field org_agenda_view_columns_initially? boolean Show column view (through Emacs) each time an agenda view is made, as `C-c C-x C-c` there would. (`org-agenda-view-columns-initially`)
---@field org_agenda_persistent_filter? boolean A new agenda view keeps the filters by tag, category, regexp and effort of the agenda still open, rather than starting without them. (`org-agenda-persistent-filter`)
---@field org_agenda_block_separator? string|false What goes between the blocks of a block agenda: one character repeated across 79 columns, a longer text as it is, or `false` for nothing. In a block's `settings`, the separator above that block. (`org-agenda-block-separator`)
---@field org_agenda_compact_blocks? boolean Leave out the separators between blocks and the header over agenda blocks. (`org-agenda-compact-blocks`)
---@field agenda_hide_empty_blocks? boolean Leave out the blocks of a block agenda that show no entries. Not in Org.
---@field org_super_agenda_mode? boolean org-super-agenda: show the lines of the agenda's views in the groups of `org_super_agenda_groups`, each under a header. Off, the groups are not used. (`org-super-agenda-mode`)
---@field org_super_agenda_groups? (table|any[])[] org-super-agenda's groups, in turn taking the lines they select: each an object of selectors and their arguments in Emacs's order, `{ name = "Today", time-grid = true }`, or a list of them in turn; `true` is `t`, `false` is `nil` (and for `name`, `none`: no header). The lines no group takes come last, under `org_super_agenda_unmatched_name`. In a custom command's `settings`, the groups of that command or block. (`org-super-agenda-groups`)
---@field org_super_agenda_unmatched_name? string The header of the lines no group takes. (`org-super-agenda-unmatched-name`)
---@field org_super_agenda_unmatched_order? integer Where the lines no group takes come among the groups: groups show lowest `order` first. (`org-super-agenda-unmatched-order`)
---@field org_super_agenda_header_separator? string What goes before each header: text, or one character repeated across the window, then a newline. (`org-super-agenda-header-separator`)
---@field org_super_agenda_header_prefix? string What each header starts with. (`org-super-agenda-header-prefix`)
---@field org_super_agenda_final_group_separator? string What goes after the last group: text put at the end of its last line, or one character repeated across the window on a line of its own. (`org-super-agenda-final-group-separator`)
---@field org_super_agenda_date_format? string The headers of the groups of `auto-planning` and `auto-ts`, as `format-time-string` writes dates. (`org-super-agenda-date-format`)
---@field org_super_agenda_hide_empty_groups? boolean Leave out the headers of groups whose lines the agenda's filters all hide. (`org-super-agenda-hide-empty-groups`)
---@field org_super_agenda_keep_order? boolean Sort the lines of each group again, as the view sorts its lines: a group of several selectors puts the lines of its first selector first. (`org-super-agenda-keep-order`)
---@field org_super_agenda_group_property_name? string The property `auto-group` groups lines by. (`org-super-agenda-group-property-name`)
---@field org_super_agenda_properties_inherit? boolean Look for the properties of `property`, `auto-property` and `auto-group` in the entries above too. (`org-super-agenda-properties-inherit`)
---@field org_roam_enabled? boolean org-roam: the files of `org_roam_directory` are a network of notes, whose nodes (files and entries with an `ID`) are found, linked to and made with the `org.roam.*` commands. Off, those commands only say so.
---@field org_roam_directory? string The directory of org-roam's files, in all its subdirectories. `~/` is the home directory. (`org-roam-directory`)
---@field org_roam_file_extensions? string[] The extensions of org-roam's files; each may be followed by `.gpg` or `.age`, which the server cannot read. (`org-roam-file-extensions`)
---@field org_roam_file_exclude_regexp? string|string[] Emacs regexps for the files that are not org-roam's, matched against their names relative to `org_roam_directory`; the default leaves out attachments. (`org-roam-file-exclude-regexp`)
---@field org_roam_node_display_template? string How a node is offered: `${field}` for its `title`, `tags`, `todo`, `file`, `olp`, `aliases` and the other fields of `org-roam-node`, `${field:N}` in N columns, `${field:*}` in what is left of the window's 79 columns. (`org-roam-node-display-template`)
---@field org_roam_node_formatter? string The description of a link `org.roam.node.insert` makes, a template as `org_roam_node_display_template` is (`${title} (${file})`); empty for the node's title. (`org-roam-node-formatter`)
---@field org_roam_db_extra_links_elements? ('node-property'|'keyword')[] Where else org-roam finds links than where Org does: in property drawers (`node-property`) and keyword lines (`keyword`). (`org-roam-db-extra-links-elements`)
---@field org_roam_db_extra_links_exclude_keys? table By `node-property` and `keyword`, the properties and keywords whose links are not links, in any case. A list given replaces the default one. (`org-roam-db-extra-links-exclude-keys`)
---@field org_roam_graph_executable? string The Graphviz program that draws `org.roam.graph`'s graph, found on `PATH` or a path. (`org-roam-graph-executable`)
---@field org_roam_graph_viewer? string The program that shows the graph, given its file. (`org-roam-graph-viewer`)
---@field org_roam_graph_filetype? string What Graphviz draws the graph as (its `-T`): `svg`, `png`, `pdf`… (`org-roam-graph-filetype`)
---@field org_roam_graph_extra_config? table Graphviz's attributes of the graph, in their order: `{ "rankdir": "LR" }`. (`org-roam-graph-extra-config`)
---@field org_roam_graph_edge_extra_config? table Graphviz's attributes of the links: `{ "dir": "back" }`. (`org-roam-graph-edge-extra-config`)
---@field org_roam_graph_node_extra_config? table Graphviz's attributes of the nodes, by the type of the link to them: `id` for org-roam's nodes, `https` for the web pages linked to. (`org-roam-graph-node-extra-config`)
---@field org_roam_graph_link_hidden_types? string[] The types of links the graph leaves out. (`org-roam-graph-link-hidden-types`)
---@field org_roam_graph_max_title_length? integer The width a title takes in the graph, at most. (`org-roam-graph-max-title-length`)
---@field org_roam_graph_shorten_titles? 'truncate'|'wrap'|false What becomes of a title wider than `org_roam_graph_max_title_length`: cut with `...` (`truncate`), over lines (`wrap`), or nothing (`false`). (`org-roam-graph-shorten-titles`)
---@field org_roam_extract_new_file_path? string The file `org.roam.extract` offers for a node, relative to `org_roam_directory`: `%<…>` is the time as `format-time-string` writes it, `${title}`, `${slug}` and `${id}` the node's. (`org-roam-extract-new-file-path`)
---@field org_roam_link_auto_replace? boolean Turn the `roam:` links of org-roam's files into `id:` links to their nodes as the files are saved, and as a link is followed. (`org-roam-link-auto-replace`)
---@field org_roam_completion_everywhere? boolean In org-roam's files, offer the titles of the nodes for the word at the cursor, not only in a link; the one picked becomes a link to its node. (`org-roam-completion-everywhere`)
---@field org_roam_mode_sections? ('backlinks'|'reflinks'|'unlinked-references')[] The sections of the org-roam buffer, in turn: `backlinks` (the nodes that link to the node), `reflinks` (those that link to or cite its references) and `unlinked-references` (the places that name it without a link, found by the server itself). (`org-roam-mode-sections`)
---@field org_roam_capture_templates? org_lsp.RoamCaptureTemplate[] The templates of new nodes, as `org_capture_templates` writes them, with org-roam's targets: `file` and a path, `file+head`, a path and the text a new file starts with, `file+olp`, a path and the headlines of an outline path, `file+head+olp`, both, and `node` and the title, alias or `ID` of a node. The path, relative to `org_roam_directory`, and the texts may have `${title}`, `${slug}`, `${id}` and `${name}`, which asks for `name` (`${name=default}` starts the answer with `default`), besides the escapes of `org_capture_templates`. `unnarrowed` shows the whole file in the capture buffer. (`org-roam-capture-templates`)
---@field org_roam_dailies_directory? string Where the daily notes are, relative to `org_roam_directory`. (`org-roam-dailies-directory`)
---@field org_roam_dailies_capture_templates? org_lsp.RoamCaptureTemplate[] The templates of the daily notes, as `org_roam_capture_templates` writes them, with paths relative to `org_roam_dailies_directory`; `%<…>` is the note's day. (`org-roam-dailies-capture-templates`)
---@field org_journal_enabled? boolean org-journal: a journal in `org_journal_dir`, a file a day (or week, month, year), written with the `org.journal.*` commands. Off, those commands only say so.
---@field org_journal_dir? string The directory of the journal's files. `~/` is the home directory. (`org-journal-dir`)
---@field org_journal_file_type? 'daily'|'weekly'|'monthly'|'yearly' A file a day (`daily`), week (`weekly`), month (`monthly`) or year (`yearly`); but daily, each entry has its date in a `CREATED` property. (`org-journal-file-type`)
---@field org_journal_start_on_weekday? integer The day a weekly file starts on: 1 is Monday, 7 Sunday. (`org-journal-start-on-weekday`)
---@field org_journal_file_format? string The names of the files, as `format-time-string` writes the date their entries start on: with `%Y`, `%m` and `%d` (a yearly file needs only `%Y`, a monthly one `%Y` and `%m`). A name without `.org` is an Org file to org-journal, but not to most editors. (`org-journal-file-format`)
---@field org_journal_date_format? string The date of an entry, after `org_journal_date_prefix`, as `format-time-string` writes it, in the C locale: `%x` is `10/09/26`. (`org-journal-date-format`)
---@field org_journal_date_prefix? string What comes before the date of an entry: a headline, or `#+title: ` in a daily file. (`org-journal-date-prefix`)
---@field org_journal_time_format? string The time of a new entry of the day, as `format-time-string` writes it; empty for none. (`org-journal-time-format`)
---@field org_journal_time_format_post_midnight? string The time of an entry made after midnight while it is still yesterday (`org_extend_today_until`); empty for `org_journal_time_format`. (`org-journal-time-format-post-midnight`)
---@field org_journal_time_prefix? string What comes before the time of an entry: its stars give its level. (`org-journal-time-prefix`)
---@field org_journal_hide_entries_p? boolean Fold all but the new entry. (`org-journal-hide-entries-p`)
---@field org_journal_follow_mode? boolean In the calendar (`org.goto.calendar`), the entry of the day `]` and `[` move to shows in another window. (`org-journal-follow-mode`)
---@field org_journal_find_file_fn? 'find-file-other-window'|'find-file' Where a new entry and today's file (`org.journal.open.current`) show: in another window, or (`"find-file"`) in the window of the command. (`org-journal-find-file-fn`)
---@field org_journal_carryover_items? string The items of the entry before that move to today's, with the headlines above them: a match of the tags view (`m`); empty for none. (`org-journal-carryover-items`)
---@field org_journal_skip_carryover_drawers? string[] Drawers left out of the items carried over. (`org-journal-skip-carryover-drawers`)
---@field org_journal_carryover_delete_empty_journal? 'never'|'always'|'ask' What happens to an entry or file left empty by the carryover: `never` deleted, `always`, or `ask`. (`org-journal-carryover-delete-empty-journal`)
---@field org_journal_file_header? string What a new file starts with, as `format-time-string` writes it: `#+title: Journal %Y`. (`org-journal-file-header`)
---@field org_journal_created_property_timestamp_format? string The date in the `CREATED` property of an entry of a file that is not daily. (`org-journal-created-property-timestamp-format`)
---@field org_journal_scheduled_string? string What comes before the timestamp of an entry for a day to come (`org.journal.new.scheduled.entry`). (`org-journal-scheduled-string`)
---@field org_journal_enable_encryption? boolean Tag the date of a new entry with `crypt`, for `org-crypt` to encrypt (`org.crypt.encrypt.all`, or as the file is saved with `org-crypt-use-before-save-magic` in the Emacs configuration). (`org-journal-enable-encryption`)
---@field org_journal_enable_agenda_integration? boolean Add the journal's files of today and the days to come to the agenda's files. (`org-journal-enable-agenda-integration`)
---@field org_journal_search_result_date_format? string The date of each result of a search of the journal, as `format-time-string` writes it. (`org-journal-search-result-date-format`)
---@field org_journal_search_results_order_by? 'asc'|'desc' The order of the results of a search of the journal: `asc`, the first day first, or `desc`, the last. (`org-journal-search-results-order-by`)
---@field org_extend_today_until? integer The hour before which it is still yesterday: for the agenda's today, the date prompt with nothing to start from, the times capture templates put in and the day of a datetree, `.+` and `++` repeaters, clock report ranges and org-journal's new entries. (`org-extend-today-until`)
---@field org_use_effective_time? boolean Before the hour `org_extend_today_until`, a TODO change records 23:59 of the day before: in `CLOSED:`, `LAST_REPEAT` and the notes' timestamps. `org.todo.yesterday` does so at any hour. (`org-use-effective-time`)
---@field org_use_last_clock_out_time_as_effective_time? boolean A TODO change records the end of the first closed clock of the subtree instead of the time now, when there is one; this comes before `org_use_effective_time`. (`org-use-last-clock-out-time-as-effective-time`)
---@field org_agenda_search_view_always_boolean? boolean Read every search string of the search view (`s`) as words that must (`+word`, `word`) or must not (`-word`) occur, regexps in braces (`{regexp}`) and `"quoted phrases"`. Off, it is a phrase whose spaces match any whitespace, unless its first word starts with `+`, `-` or `{`. (`org-agenda-search-view-always-boolean`)
---@field org_agenda_search_view_force_full_words? boolean The words of a Boolean search match whole words only, as a search string starting with `:` asks. (`org-agenda-search-view-force-full-words`)
---@field org_agenda_search_view_max_outline_level? integer The search view shows headlines down to this level, a match below one counting for the headline above: 0 for every level. (`org-agenda-search-view-max-outline-level`)
---@field org_agenda_text_search_extra_files? string[] Files the search view (`s`) searches besides the agenda files, after them, as `org_agenda_files` names them. `agenda-archives` first adds the files the agenda files archive to. A file listed twice is searched at its last place. (`org-agenda-text-search-extra-files`)
---@field org_agenda_prefix_format? table What comes before the headline on a line of the agenda (`agenda`), the TODO list (`todo`), tags searches (`tags`) and text searches (`search`): `%c` the category, `%t` the time, `%s` the leader (`Scheduled: `), `%e` the effort, `%l` a blank per level, `%T` the last tag, `%b` the headlines above (breadcrumbs), `%i` the icon (none). A width pads (`%-12c` on the right, `%12c` on the left), `.N` cuts, `%?` leaves out an empty field, and a character before the letter follows a value that is not empty (`%-12:c`). `%(…)` is Emacs Lisp, run by Emacs (the `emacs` option) on the entry's headline with `category`, `extra`, `level` … bound, as Org runs it: `%(org-entry-get nil "X")`, or a function of `emacs_config`. Without Emacs it is empty. (`org-agenda-prefix-format`)
---@field org_agenda_todo_keyword_format? string How a TODO keyword is written in the agenda: `%-4s` pads it to four columns, so that the titles line up; an empty format leaves keywords out. (`org-agenda-todo-keyword-format`)
---@field org_agenda_scheduled_leaders? string[] The leader of a scheduled entry on its day, and on today when its day has passed (`%d`: the days since). (`org-agenda-scheduled-leaders`)
---@field org_agenda_deadline_leaders? string[] The leader of a deadline on its day, on today before it (`%d`: the days left), and on today after it (`%d`: the days since). (`org-agenda-deadline-leaders`)
---@field org_agenda_deadline_faces? any[][] The look of a deadline in the agenda by the part of its warning time that has passed: the first whose part is no more than that, none past the last. The faces are Org's: `org-imminent-deadline` (or `org-warning`), `org-upcoming-deadline`, `org-upcoming-distant-deadline`; any other name is the plain look. (`org-agenda-deadline-faces`)
---@field org_agenda_timerange_leaders? string[] The leader of a time range within a day, and of a range over days (`%d`: which day of it, `%d`: how many days). (`org-agenda-timerange-leaders`)
---@field org_agenda_inactive_leader? string What goes before an inactive timestamp's line, when the agenda shows them (`[`, `v [`). (`org-agenda-inactive-leader`)
---@field org_agenda_remove_tags? true|false|'prefix' Leave the tags out of the agenda's lines; `prefix` only when the prefix shows a tag (`%T`). (`org-agenda-remove-tags`)
---@field org_stuck_projects? any[] What the stuck projects view (`#`) lists: the projects the match string finds whose subtree has no entry with one of the TODO keywords (`*` for any not done) or tags (`*` for any), and no match of the regexp. (`org-stuck-projects`)
---@field org_agenda_breadcrumbs_separator? string What goes between the headlines of `%b`. (`org-agenda-breadcrumbs-separator`)
---@field org_agenda_format_date? string How the agenda writes a day, as `format-time-string` does: `%A %d %B %Y`, `%a %Y-%m-%d`. (`org-agenda-format-date`)
---@field org_agenda_start_day? string The day the agenda starts from, as the date prompt reads it: `-1d`, `+2d`, `2026-10-01`. (`org-agenda-start-day`)
---@field org_agenda_custom_commands_contexts? table[] Where the commands of `org_agenda_custom_commands` are offered, as for `org_capture_templates_contexts`. (`org-agenda-custom-commands-contexts`)
---@field org_agenda_custom_commands? org_lsp.AgendaCommand[] Commands of the agenda menu. Each has a `key` and a `description`, and either a `type` (`agenda`, `alltodo`, `todo`, `tags` or `tags-todo`) with a `match` (keywords such as `NEXT|WAIT` for `todo`, a match string such as `work-home` or `PRIORITY="A"` for `tags`), or `blocks`, a list of such views shown one after the other. `header` replaces a view's header (empty: none), and `settings` holds agenda options for the command or a block (`org_agenda_span`, `org_agenda_start_day` …). A command with only a key and a description groups the commands whose keys start with its key. (`org-agenda-custom-commands`)

---@class org_lsp.RefileTarget
---@field files? 'current'|'agenda'|'org-id-files'|string|string[] `current` (the entry's own file), `agenda`, `org-id-files` (the files with IDs), a path or a list of paths
---@field level? integer The headlines of this level
---@field maxlevel? integer The headlines up to this level
---@field tag? string The headlines with this tag
---@field todo? string The headlines with this TODO keyword
---@field regexp? string The headlines that match this regular expression
---@field all? boolean Every headline

---@class org_lsp.CaptureTemplate
---@field key string The keys that pick the template
---@field description string What the menu shows
---@field type? 'entry'|'plain'|'item'|'checkitem'|'table-line' An Org entry (the default), plain text, a list item, a checkbox item or a table line
---@field target? string[] `file` and a path; `file+headline`, a path and a headline; `file+olp`, a path and the headlines of an outline path; `file+regexp`, a path and a regular expression; `file+olp+datetree`, a path and maybe an outline path; `id` and an ID; `clock`; `file+function`, a path and an Emacs Lisp function; or `function` and an Emacs Lisp function that visits the file
---@field template? string|string[] The template, or its lines
---@field prepend? boolean First under the target, not last
---@field empty_lines? integer Blank lines before and after
---@field empty_lines_before? integer Blank lines before
---@field empty_lines_after? integer Blank lines after
---@field immediate_finish? boolean Capture without a capture buffer
---@field tree_type? 'day'|'week'|'month'|('year'|'quarter'|'month'|'week'|'day')[] The levels of a date tree
---@field time_prompt? boolean Ask for the date of the date tree entry
---@field table_line_pos? string Where a table line goes: `II-1` is above the second rule
---@field clock_in? boolean Clock the captured entry while the capture goes on
---@field clock_keep? boolean Keep the clock running when the capture is filed
---@field clock_resume? boolean Start the clock the capture stopped again
---@field jump_to_captured? boolean Show the captured entry in its file
---@field unnarrowed? boolean Show the whole target file in the capture buffer

---@class org_lsp.RoamCaptureTemplate
---@field key string The keys that pick the template
---@field description string What the menu shows
---@field type? 'entry'|'plain'|'item'|'checkitem'|'table-line' An Org entry (the default), plain text, a list item, a checkbox item or a table line
---@field target? string[] `file` and a path; `file+head`, a path and the head of a new file; `file+olp`, a path and an outline path; `file+head+olp`, a path, a head and an outline path; or `node` and a node's title, alias or ID
---@field template? string|string[] The template, or its lines
---@field prepend? boolean First under the target, not last
---@field empty_lines? integer Blank lines before and after
---@field empty_lines_before? integer Blank lines before
---@field empty_lines_after? integer Blank lines after
---@field unnarrowed? boolean Show the whole file in the capture buffer

---@class org_lsp.AgendaCommand
---@field key string The keys that pick the command in the agenda menu
---@field description string What the menu shows
---@field type? 'agenda'|'alltodo'|'todo'|'tags'|'tags-todo'|'search'|'stuck' The view
---@field match? string For todo, keywords (NEXT|WAIT); for tags, a match string (work-home, PRIORITY="A")
---@field header? string Replaces the view's header; empty for none
---@field settings? table Agenda options for this command: org_agenda_span, org_agenda_start_day…
---@field blocks? org_lsp.AgendaBlock[] Views shown one after the other

---@class org_lsp.AgendaBlock
---@field type 'agenda'|'alltodo'|'todo'|'tags'|'tags-todo'|'search'|'stuck' The view
---@field match? string For todo, keywords; for tags, a match string
---@field header? string Replaces the view's header; empty for none
---@field settings? table Agenda options for this block: org_agenda_span, org_agenda_start_day…
